                                <div class="main-content" >
                                    <section data-title="Introduction"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec1">Introduction</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec1-content"><p>Polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHAs) are biopolymers classified as polyesters which many bacteria and archaea accumulate in the form of intracellular granules as a reservoir of energy and carbon in response to stress conditions, especially when nitrogen is limited [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Hazenberg W, Witholt B (1997) Efficient production of medium-chain-length poly(3-hydroxyalkanoates) from octane by Pseudomonas oleovorans: economic considerations. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 48:5: 588–596. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s002530051100&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR1" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e383">1</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Lenz RW, Marchessault RH (2005) Bacterial Polyesters: Biosynthesis, Biodegradable Plastics and Biotechnology. Biomacromolecules 6:1: 1–8. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/bm049700c&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR2" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e383_1">2</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Stainer W R, Kunisawa R, Contopoulou B (1959) The role of organic substrates in bacterial photosynthesis. Proc Natl Acad Sci 45:1246–1260" href="#ref-CR3" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e383_2">3</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 4" title="Poli A, Di Donato P, Abbamondi GR, Nicolaus B (2011) Synthesis, Production, and Biotechnological Applications of Exopolysaccharides and Polyhydroxyalkanoates by Archaea, Archaea, 2011, 1: 693253, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1155/2011/693253&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR4" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e386">4</a>]. Maurice Lemoigne was the first scientist to isolate and characterize poly-3-hydroxybutyrate (P3HB), the simplest type of PHA, as a storage compound from <i>Bacillus megaterium</i> in 1926 [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2" title="Lenz RW, Marchessault RH (2005) Bacterial Polyesters: Biosynthesis, Biodegradable Plastics and Biotechnology. Biomacromolecules 6:1: 1–8. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/bm049700c&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR2" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e392">2</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 5" title="Lemoigne M (1926) Produits de deshydration et de polymerisation de l’acide B-oxybutyric, The Bulletin de la Société. Chim de France 8:770–778" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR5" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e395">5</a>]. P3HB is a polymer composed of 4 carbon-chain-length repeating monomers, and it is the most common and abundant PHA in bacteria [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 6" title="Jendrossek D, Handrick R (2002) Microbial Degradation of Polyhydroxyalkanoates. Annu Rev Microbiol 56:1: 403–432. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.micro.56.012302.160838&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e399">6</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 7" title="Steinbüchel A, Valentin HE (1995) Diversity of bacterial polyhydroxyalkanoic acids. FEMS Microbiol Lett 128:3: 219–228. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/0378-1097(95)00125-O&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR7" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e402">7</a>]. PHAs are a good source of chemicals, polymers and biofuels [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 8" title="Gao X, Chen J-C, Wu Q, Chen G-Q (2011) Polyhydroxyalkanoates as a source of chemicals, polymers, and biofuels. Curr Opin Biotechnol 22:6: 768–774. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.copbio.2011.06.005&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR8" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e405">8</a>].</p><p>Since this discovery, almost 100 years ago, thousands of papers have been published and some good reviews have been written describing the generalities of PHAs [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 9" title="Reddy CSK, Ghai R, Rashmi, Kalia VC (2003) Polyhydroxyalkanoates: an overview. Bioresour Technol 87(2):137–146. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/S0960-8524(02)00212-2&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR9" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e411">9</a>], their structure and composition diversity [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 10" title="Samui AB, Kanai T (2019) Polyhydroxyalkanoates based copolymers. Int J Biol Macromol 140:522–537. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijbiomac.2019.08.147&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR10" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e414">10</a>], applications in several fields such as: medicine [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 11" title="Grigore ME, Grigorescu RM, Iancu L, Ion R-M, Zaharia C, Andrei ER (2019) Methods of synthesis, properties and biomedical applications of polyhydroxyalkanoates: a review. J Biomater Sci Polym Ed 30:9: 695–712. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1080/09205063.2019.1605866&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR11" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e417">11</a>], agriculture, biofuels and packaging [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 12" title="Pandey A, Adama N, Adjallé K, Blais J-F (2022) Sustainable applications of polyhydroxyalkanoates in various fields: A critical review. Int J Biol Macromol 221:1184–1201. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijbiomac.2022.09.098&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR12" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e420">12</a>]. There are also reviews describing potential suitable hosts for industrial production (such as <i>Cupriavidus necator</i>,<i> Pseudomonas</i>, recombinant <i>Escherichia coli</i> and yeasts) and their metabolic pathways [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 13" title="Nagarajan D, Aristya Ganies R, Lin Y-J, Chang J-J, Yen H-W, Chang J-S (2021) Microbial cell factories for the production of polyhydroxyalkanoates. Essays Biochem 65:2: 337–353. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1042/EBC20200142&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR13" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e433">13</a>].</p><p>This review re-capitulates some of the information mentioned in these reviews [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 14" title="Rehm BHA (2003) Polyester synthases: natural catalysts for plastics. Biochem J 376:1: 15–33. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1042/bj20031254&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR14" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e439">14</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 15" title="Lee SY (1996) Bacterial polyhydroxyalkanoates, Biotechnology and Bioengineering, 49, 1: 1–14, 10.1002/(SICI)1097– 0290(19960105)49:1 < 1::AID-BIT1 &gt; 3.0.CO;2-P" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR15" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e442">15</a>], for example, main advantages and disadvantages of producing PHAs and the main metabolic pathway (β-oxidation pathway) is shown again [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 16" title="Steinbüchel A, Lütke-Eversloh T (2003) Metabolic engineering and pathway construction for biotechnological production of relevant polyhydroxyalkanoates in microorganisms. Biochem Eng J 16:2: 81–96. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/S1369-703X(03)00036-6&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR16" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e445">16</a>] and expands to the specific genes that have been knockout and/or overexpressed from this pathway to enhance PHAs accumulation. It also compiles different methods for protein engineering and enzymatic evaluation as well as classification, structure and substrate specificity of both PhaC and PhaZ enzymes.</p><p>The PHA depolymerase (PhaZ) enzyme is responsible of depolymerizing the bioplastic and can be a useful tool for bioremediation of PHAs. Although, there are a volume of studies on PhaZ, for example the PHA Depolymerase Engineering Database published by Knoll, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 17" title="Knoll M, Hamm TM, Wagner F, Martinez V, Pleiss J (2009) The PHA Depolymerase Engineering Database: A systematic analysis tool for the diverse family of polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) depolymerases. BMC Bioinformatics 10(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2105-10-89&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR17" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e451">17</a>], this volume is by far less than the volume of papers published on PhaC and review papers are greatly lacking. The reason PhaZ has been less studied than PhaC is because there isn’t sufficient need for PHAs bioremediation as PHAs accumulation in the environment is still not a problem [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 18" title="Wang Y, Yin J, Chen G-Q (2014) Polyhydroxyalkanoates, challenges and opportunities. Curr Opin Biotechnol 30:59–65. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.copbio.2014.06.001&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e454">18</a>].</p><p>Another novelty of this work is the construction of a mutation map for the amino acid sequence of the PHA synthase (PhaC) from <i>Pseudomonas putida</i>. The purpose of the map is to show several potential amino acid change mutations that exist already in nature and that could be used to change the specificity/activity of a target sequence so that polymers with different monomer compositions could be created. The map is also accompanied by a table of single amino acid mutations that have already been studied and their effect over the enzyme.</p></div></div></section><section data-title="Bioplastics"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec2-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec2">Bioplastics</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec2-content"><p>Bioplastics are a growing class of materials that can be used as an alternative to petroleum-based plastic [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 19" title="Atiwesh G, Mikhael A, Parrish CC, Banoub J, Le T-A T (2021) Environmental impact of bioplastic use: A review, Heliyon, 7, 9: e07918, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2021.e07918&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR19" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e472">19</a>]. All bioplastics are produced by living organisms from renewable feedstock. However, not all bioplastics are biodegradable [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 19" title="Atiwesh G, Mikhael A, Parrish CC, Banoub J, Le T-A T (2021) Environmental impact of bioplastic use: A review, Heliyon, 7, 9: e07918, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2021.e07918&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR19" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e475">19</a>]. The main types of bioplastics are illustrated in Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig1">1</a><b>.</b> Bioplastics have been studied for more than a century and yet, their industrialization is still embryonic [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 20" title="Fredi G, Dorigato A (2021) Recycling of bioplastic waste: A review. Adv Industrial Eng Polym Res 4:3: 159–177. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aiepr.2021.06.006&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR20" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e483">20</a>]. According to the report of the European Bioplastics, bioplastics currently (2023) represent roughly 0.5% percent of the over 400 million tonnes of plastic produced annually [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 21" title="Bioplastics E (2024) March, European Bioplastics e.V. &#xA;                  https://www.european-bioplastics.org/bioplastics/&#xA;                  &#xA;                 (accessed 11th 2024)" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR21" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e486">21</a>].</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-1" data-title="Fig. 1"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig1" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 1</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><img aria-describedby="figure-1-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig1_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 1" loading="lazy" width="685" height="575"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/figures/1" data-track-dest="link:Figure1 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 1" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-1-desc"><p>Types of bioplastics modified from [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Bhagwat G et al (2020) Benchmarking Bioplastics: A Natural Step Towards a Sustainable Future. J Polym Environ 28:12: 3055–3075. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s10924-020-01830-8&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR22" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501">22</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Das M, Manda B, Katiyar V (2020) Sustainable routes for synthesis of poly (ε-caprolactone): Prospects in chemical industries. Springer, Singapore" href="#ref-CR23" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_1">23</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Patni N, Yadava P, Agarwal A, Maroo V (2014) An overview on the role of wheat gluten as a viable substitute for biodegradable plastics, 30, 4: 421–430, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1515/revce-2013-0039&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR24" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_2">24</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Hardin T (2024) February,. Plastic: It’s Not All the Same. Copyright © Plastic Oceans International. &#xA;                  https://plasticoceans.org/7-types-of-plastic/&#xA;                  &#xA;                 (accessed" href="#ref-CR25" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_3">25</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Zhou Y, He Y, Lin X, Feng Y, Liu M (2022) Sustainable, High-Performance, and Biodegradable Plastics Made from Chitin. ACS Appl Mater Interfaces 14:41: 46980–46993. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/acsami.2c12764&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR26" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_4">26</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Chen G-Q, Chen X-Y, Wu F-Q, Chen J-C (2020) Polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA) toward cost competitiveness and functionality. Adv Industrial Eng Polym Res 3:1: 1–7. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aiepr.2019.11.001&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR27" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_5">27</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Jian J, Xiangbin Z, Xianbo H (2020) An overview on synthesis, properties and applications of poly(butylene-adipate-co-terephthalate)–PBAT. Adv Industrial Eng Polym Res 3:1: 19–26. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aiepr.2020.01.001&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR28" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_6">28</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Rishi V, Sandhu AK, Kaur A, Kaur J, Sharma S, Soni SK (2020) Utilization of kitchen waste for production of pullulan to develop biodegradable plastic. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 104:3: 1307–1317. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-019-10167-9&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR29" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_7">29</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Wang J, Wang L, Gardner DJ, Shaler SM, Cai Z (2021) Towards a cellulose-based society: opportunities and challenges, Cellulose, 28, 8: 4511–4543, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s10570-021-03771-4&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR30" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_8">30</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Gheorghita R, Gutt G, Amariei S The Use of Edible Films Based on Sodium Alginate in Meat Product Packaging: An Eco-Friendly Alternative to Conventional Plastic Materials, Coatings, vol. 10, no. 2, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3390/coatings10020166&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR31" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_9">31</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Guilbert S, Morel M-H, Gontard N, Cuq B (2006) Protein-Based Plastics and Composites as Smart Green Materials, in Feedstocks for the Future, vol. 921, (ACS Symposium Series, no. 921): American Chemical Society, ch. 24, pp. 334–350" href="#ref-CR32" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_10">32</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Merino D, Paul UC, Athanassiou A (2021) Bio-based plastic films prepared from potato peels using mild acid hydrolysis followed by plasticization with a polyglycerol. Food Packaging Shelf Life 29:100707. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fpsl.2021.100707&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR33" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_11">33</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Baker MI, Walsh SP, Schwartz Z, Boyan BD (2012) A review of polyvinyl alcohol and its uses in cartilage and orthopedic applications. J Biomedical Mater Res Part B: Appl Biomaterials 100B:5: 1451–1457. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/jbm.b.32694&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR34" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_12">34</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Jalabert M, Fraschini C, Prud’homme RE (2007) Synthesis and characterization of poly(L-lactide)s and poly(D-lactide)s of controlled molecular weight. J Polym Sci Part A: Polym Chem 45:10: 1944–1955. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/pola.21960&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR35" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_13">35</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Patnaik S, Panda AK, Kumar S (2020) Thermal degradation of corn starch based biodegradable plastic plates and determination of kinetic parameters by isoconversional methods using thermogravimetric analyzer. J Energy Inst 93:4: 1449–1459. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joei.2020.01.007&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR36" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_14">36</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Li G et al Synthesis and Biological Application of Polylactic Acid. Molecules, 25, 21, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules25215023&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR37" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_15">37</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Siracusa V, Blanco I Bio-Polyethylene (Bio-PE), Bio-Polypropylene (Bio-PP) and Bio-Poly(ethylene terephthalate) (Bio-PET): Recent Developments in Bio-Based Polymers Analogous to Petroleum-Derived Ones for Packaging and Engineering Applications, Polymers, vol. 12, no. 8, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3390/polym12081641&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR38" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e501_16">38</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 39" title="Barletta M et al (2022) Poly(butylene succinate) (PBS): Materials, processing, and industrial applications. Prog Polym Sci 132:101579. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.progpolymsci.2022.101579&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR39" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e504">39</a>]</p></div></div></figure></div><p>The main advantage of bioplastics is that they have a faster biodegradation rate than petroleum-based plastics in a wide range of environments. Common petroleum-based plastics such as polyethylene, (PE) polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene terephthalate (PET) have a lifespan of 10–600, 50–80 and up to 450 years depending on the conditions [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 40" title="Mohanan N, Montazer Z, Sharma PK, Levin DB (2020) Microbial and Enzymatic Degradation of Synthetic Plastics, (in English). Front Microbiol Rev 11. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2020.580709&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR40" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e518">40</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 41" title="Telmo O (2013) Polymers and the Environment. Polymer Science, Y. Faris Ed. IntechOpen, Rijeka. Ch. 1. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.5772/51057&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR41" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e521">41</a>] while a PHA bottle is estimated to degrade in only 1.5–3.5 years in the marine environment [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 42" title="Dilkes-Hoffman LS, Lant PA, Laycock B, Pratt S (2019) The rate of biodegradation of PHA bioplastics in the marine environment: A meta-study. Mar Pollut Bull 142:15–24. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.marpolbul.2019.03.020&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR42" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e524">42</a>]. In soil, with the appropriate temperature and moisture levels, PHA can degrade in as little as 7 weeks [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 43" title="Rudnik E (2013) 11 - Biodegradability Testing of Compostable Polymer Materials. Handbook of Biopolymers and Biodegradable Plastics. William Andrew Publishing, S. Ebnesajjad Ed. Boston, pp 213–263. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-1-4557-2834-3.00011-2&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR43" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e527">43</a>]. PHAs are also inexhaustible type of material which is an advantage over petroleum given that petroleum reserves are expected to run out by the year 2070 [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 44" title="worldometer (2024) Oil left in the world. &#xA;                  https://www.worldometers.info/oil/#:~:text=The%20world%20has%20proven%20reserves,(at%20current%20consumption%20levels&#xA;                  &#xA;                ) (accessed October 29, 2024)" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR44" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e530">44</a>].</p><p>On the other hand, one of the main roadblocks to produce bioplastics is their higher costs. It has been calculated that the minimum selling price of PHAs in 2022 was 4.83 $/kg [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 45" title="Rajendran N, Han J (2022) Techno-economic analysis of food waste valorization for integrated production of polyhydroxyalkanoates and biofuels. Bioresour Technol 348:126796. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biortech.2022.126796&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR45" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e536">45</a>] while the global cost of, for example, high density polyethylene (HDPE) and of polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is ≈ 1 $/kg [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 46" title="Statita Statista. Price of high-density polyethylene worldwide from 2017 to 2023. (accessed October 29, 2024) &#xA;                  https://www.statista.com/statistics/1171074/price-high-density-polyethylene-forecast-globally/&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR46" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e539">46</a>]. The high costs of PHA production comes mainly from the carbon feedstock used for the fermentation (≈ 38% of the production costs) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 47" title="Choi J, Lee SY (1999) Factors affecting the economics of polyhydroxyalkanoate production by bacterial fermentation. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 51:1: 13–21. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s002530051357&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR47" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e542">47</a>]. Common industrial feedstocks include sugar beet, sugar cane bioproduct, canola oil, corn or cassava and sucrose [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Technavio (2017) Top 6 vendors in the polyhydroxyalkanoate market from 2017 to 2021:Technavio, [Online]. Available: &#xA;                  https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20170824005079/en/Top-6-Vendors-Polyhydroxyalkanoate-Market-2017-2021&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR48" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e545">48</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Kourmentza C et al (2017) Recent Advances and Challenges towards Sustainable Polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) Production. Bioengineering 4(2):103390bioengineering4020055" href="#ref-CR49" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e545_1">49</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Jiang G et al (2016) Carbon Sources for Polyhydroxyalkanoates and an Integrated Biorefinery, (in eng). Int J Mol Sci 17(1157). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms17071157&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR50" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e545_2">50</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Bio-on (2019) Bio-on. &#xA;                  http://www.bio-on.it/production.php#p9&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR51" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e545_3">51</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Danimerscientific (2019) accessed october 17,. Danimer scientific. &#xA;                  https://danimerscientific.com/pha-the-future-of-biopolymers/pha-culture-manufacturing/&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR52" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e545_4">52</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="TianAn (2019) accessed october 17,. TianAn Biopolymer. &#xA;                  http://www.tianan-enmat.com/index.html#&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR53" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e545_5">53</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="TianjinGreenBio (2019) Tiajin GreenBio Materials. &#xA;                  http://www.tjgreenbio.com/en/about.aspx?title=About%20GreenBio&amp;cid=25&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR54" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e545_6">54</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 55" title="Chen G-Q (2009) A microbial polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA) based bio- and materials industry, Chemical Society Reviews, 10.1039/B812677C 38, 8: 2434–2446, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1039/B812677C&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR55" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e548">55</a>]. Other high costs come from the fermentation itself and from downstream processing [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 56" title="Ray S (2022) Mass Multiplication, Production Cost Analysis and Marketing of Polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHAs), in Industrial Microbiology Based Entrepreneurship: Making Money from Microbes, N. Amaresan, D. Dharumadurai, and D. R. Cundell Eds. Singapore: Springer Nature Singapore, pp. 117–125" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR56" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e552">56</a>].</p><p>Additional disadvantages include the use of land to grow these feedstocks, and thermophysical properties of some types of these plastics such as thermal instability, brittleness, low melt strength etcetera [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 57" title="Jabeen N, Majid I, Nayik GA (2015) Bioplastics and food packaging: A review. Cogent Food Agric 1(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1080/23311932.2015.1117749&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR57" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e559">57</a>].</p><p><b>Classification thermophysical characteristics and advantages of PHAs</b></p><p>PHA polymers are classified by their monomer length as short chain length (scl), which normally contain 5 or less than 5 carbons (≤ C5), for example poly-3-hydroxyvalerate (P3HV) (P3HB falls in this category as well) and as medium-chain-length (mcl) which contain in between 6 and 14 carbons (≥ C6, ≤C14), for example: poly-3-hydroxyoctanoate (P3HO) and poly-3-hydroxyhexanoate (P3HHX) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Noda I, Green PR, Satkowski MM, Schechtman LA (2005) Preparation and Properties of a Novel Class of Polyhydroxyalkanoate Copolymers, Biomacromolecules, 6, 2: 580–586, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/bm049472m&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR58" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e569">58</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Samrot AV, Avinesh RB, Sukeetha SD, Senthilkumar P (2011) Accumulation of Poly[(R)-3-hydroxyalkanoates] in Enterobacter cloacae SU-1 During Growth with Two Different Carbon Sources in Batch Culture. Appl Biochem Biotechnol 163:1: 195–203. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s12010-010-9028-7&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR59" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e569_1">59</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 60" title="Kessler B, Weusthius R, Witholt B, Eggink G (2001) Production of microbial polyesters: fermentation and downstream processes. Adv Biochem Engineering/ Biotechnol 71:159–182" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR60" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e572">60</a>]. Long chain length polyhydroxyalkanoates (lcl) contain more than 14 carbons and are a very rare type of PHA [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 61" title="Luengo JM, Garcı́a B, Sandoval A, Naharro G, Olivera EaR (2003) Bioplastics from microorganisms. Curr Opin Microbiol 6(3):251–260. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/S1369-5274(03)00040-7&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR61" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e575">61</a>]. PHA polymers are composed of a backbone and a lateral chain (R) which is normally an alkane chain (Refer Fig.<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig2"> 2</a>) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 15" title="Lee SY (1996) Bacterial polyhydroxyalkanoates, Biotechnology and Bioengineering, 49, 1: 1–14, 10.1002/(SICI)1097– 0290(19960105)49:1 < 1::AID-BIT1 &gt; 3.0.CO;2-P" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR15" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e581">15</a>].</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-2" data-title="Fig. 2"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig2" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 2</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig2_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-2-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig2_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 2" loading="lazy" width="685" height="601"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/figures/2" data-track-dest="link:Figure2 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 2" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-2-desc"><p><i>PHAs general formula</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 15" title="Lee SY (1996) Bacterial polyhydroxyalkanoates, Biotechnology and Bioengineering, 49, 1: 1–14, 10.1002/(SICI)1097– 0290(19960105)49:1 < 1::AID-BIT1 &gt; 3.0.CO;2-P" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR15" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e598">15</a>]. <i>Structure modelled using ChemDraw</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 62" title="Evans DA (2014) History of the Harvard ChemDraw Project, Angewandte Chemie International Edition, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/anie.201405820&#xA;                  &#xA;                , 42: 11140–11145." href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR62" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e604">62</a>]</p></div></div></figure></div><p>Aside from alkanes, PHAs can have lateral chains containing double or triple bonds and /or include different functional groups such as halogen atoms, methoxy, benzoyl, ethoxy, cyanophenoxy, acetoxy, phenoxy, hydroxyl, nitrophenyl, epoxy, carbonyl, cyano and others [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 63" title="Olivera ER, Arcos M, Naharro G, Luengo JM, Unusual (2010) PHA Biosynthesis. In: Chen GG-Q (ed) Plastics from Bacteria: Natural Functions and Applications. Springer Berlin Heidelberg, Berlin, Heidelberg, pp 133–186" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR63" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e618">63</a>].</p><p>There are PHAs in which the length of the backbone is longer, so the hydroxyl group to be esterified is not in carbon C3. Other PHAs have a thioester group in the place of the oxoester linkage. Some of these PHAs are uncommon and can only be obtained by chemical or physical modification of naturally occurring ones [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 7" title="Steinbüchel A, Valentin HE (1995) Diversity of bacterial polyhydroxyalkanoic acids. FEMS Microbiol Lett 128:3: 219–228. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/0378-1097(95)00125-O&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR7" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e624">7</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 63" title="Olivera ER, Arcos M, Naharro G, Luengo JM, Unusual (2010) PHA Biosynthesis. In: Chen GG-Q (ed) Plastics from Bacteria: Natural Functions and Applications. Springer Berlin Heidelberg, Berlin, Heidelberg, pp 133–186" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR63" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e627">63</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 64" title="Chen G-Q, Wu Q (2005) Microbial production and applications of chiral hydroxyalkanoates. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 67:5: 592–599. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-005-1917-2&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR64" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e630">64</a>].</p><p>PHAs can also be classified as homopolymers and copolymers, for example P(3HB-<i>co</i>−3HV) is a copolymer made up from 3-hydroxybutyrate and 3-hydroxyvalerate monomers. The monomers are usually incorporated randomly across the polymer, thus they are also referred as random co-polymers [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 65" title="Wang Y, Chung A, Chen G-Q (2017) Synthesis of Medium-Chain-Length Polyhydroxyalkanoate Homopolymers, Random Copolymers, and Block Copolymers by an Engineered Strain of Pseudomonas entomophila. Adv Healthc Mater 6:7: 1601017. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/adhm.201601017&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR65" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e640">65</a>]. This process depends on the carbon substrate, metabolic pathways available and on the specificity of the enzymes involved [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 66" title="Lu J, Tappel R, Nomura C (2009) Mini-Review: Biosynthesis of Poly(hydroxyalkanoates). J Macromolecular Science® Part C: Polym Reviews 226–248. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1080/15583720903048243&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR66" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e643">66</a>]. <i>Cupriavidus</i> and other organisms with PhaC type I use acyl-CoA from the metabolism of sugars to polymerize scl-PHA [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 14" title="Rehm BHA (2003) Polyester synthases: natural catalysts for plastics. Biochem J 376:1: 15–33. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1042/bj20031254&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR14" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e649">14</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Matsusaki H, Abe H, Doi Y (2000) Biosynthesis and Properties of Poly(3-hydroxybutyrate-co-3-hydroxyalkanoates) by Recombinant Strains of Pseudomonas sp. 61– 3, Biomacromolecules, 1, 1: 17–22, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/bm9900040&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR67" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e653">67</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Tsuge T, Hyakutake M, Mizuno K (2015) Class IV polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) synthases and PHA-producing Bacillus. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 99:15: 6231–6240. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-015-6777-9&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR68" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e653_1">68</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Altschul SF et al (1997) Gapped BLAST and PSI-BLAST: a new generation of protein database search programs. Nucleic Acids Res 25:17: 3389–3402. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/25.17.3389&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR69" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e653_2">69</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 70" title="Liu Z et al (2019) Domain-centric dissection and classification of prokaryotic poly(3-hydroxyalkanoate) synthases. bioRxiv 693432. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1101/693432&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR70" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e656">70</a>] through the 3 enzymes from the operon phaCAB [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 66" title="Lu J, Tappel R, Nomura C (2009) Mini-Review: Biosynthesis of Poly(hydroxyalkanoates). J Macromolecular Science® Part C: Polym Reviews 226–248. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1080/15583720903048243&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR66" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e659">66</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 71" title="Thomson N, Roy I, Summers D, Sivaniah E (2010) In vitro production of polyhydroxyalkanoates: achievements and applications. J Chem Technol Biotechnol 85:6: 760–767. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/jctb.2299&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR71" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e662">71</a>] while organisms with PhaC type II, type III and type IV [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 14" title="Rehm BHA (2003) Polyester synthases: natural catalysts for plastics. Biochem J 376:1: 15–33. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1042/bj20031254&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR14" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e665">14</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Matsusaki H, Abe H, Doi Y (2000) Biosynthesis and Properties of Poly(3-hydroxybutyrate-co-3-hydroxyalkanoates) by Recombinant Strains of Pseudomonas sp. 61– 3, Biomacromolecules, 1, 1: 17–22, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/bm9900040&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR67" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e668">67</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Tsuge T, Hyakutake M, Mizuno K (2015) Class IV polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) synthases and PHA-producing Bacillus. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 99:15: 6231–6240. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-015-6777-9&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR68" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e668_1">68</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Altschul SF et al (1997) Gapped BLAST and PSI-BLAST: a new generation of protein database search programs. Nucleic Acids Res 25:17: 3389–3402. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/25.17.3389&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR69" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e668_2">69</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 70" title="Liu Z et al (2019) Domain-centric dissection and classification of prokaryotic poly(3-hydroxyalkanoate) synthases. bioRxiv 693432. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1101/693432&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR70" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e672">70</a>] use the intermediate 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA from the metabolism of fatty acids (β-oxidation pathway) to produce mcl-PHA [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 66" title="Lu J, Tappel R, Nomura C (2009) Mini-Review: Biosynthesis of Poly(hydroxyalkanoates). J Macromolecular Science® Part C: Polym Reviews 226–248. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1080/15583720903048243&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR66" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e675">66</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 71" title="Thomson N, Roy I, Summers D, Sivaniah E (2010) In vitro production of polyhydroxyalkanoates: achievements and applications. J Chem Technol Biotechnol 85:6: 760–767. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/jctb.2299&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR71" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e678">71</a>].</p><p>Nevertheless, It is also important to mention that, while the natural substrate of PhaC in <i>C. necator</i> is 3HB-CoA, it was able to accept 3-hydroxyoctanoate-CoA and 3-hydroxydodecanoate-CoA to produce medium-chain-length PHA when expressed in <i>E. coli</i> suggesting that the substrate specificity of the different types of PhaCs is less strict than originally thought [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 72" title="Antonio RV, Steinbüchel A, Rehm BHA (2000) Analysis of in vivo substrate specificity of the PHA synthase from Ralstonia eutropha: formation of novel copolyesters in recombinant Escherichia coli. FEMS Microbiol Lett 182(1):111–117. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1574-6968.2000.tb08883.x&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR72" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e690">72</a>]. This leads us to believe that the type of PHA (scl or mcl) is not only dependent on the substrate specificity of the PhaC but also on the specificity of the other enzymes involved and the metabolic pathway used.</p><p>Carbon feedstock is also a major factor defining the polymer composition. For example, <i>Klebsiella</i> spp. can produce P(3HB-<i>co</i>−3HV) with a higher portion of 3HV using soy waste and with a lower portion of 3HV using malt wastes as carbon feedstock, while <i>Alcaligenes latus</i> and <i>Staphylococcus</i> spp. can only produce homopolymers of hydroxybutyrate using these same substrates [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 73" title="Wong PAL, Chua H, Lo W, Lawford HG, Yu PH (2002) Production of specific copolymers of polyhydroxyalkanoates from industrial waste. Appl Biochem Biotechnol J article 98(1385/abab):1: 655–662. 10" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR73" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e708">73</a>]. In contrast, <i>Cupriavidus necator</i> (<i>Alcaligenes eutrophus)</i> is capable of increasing the proportion of HV inside P(3HB-<i>co</i>−3HV) when NH<sub>4</sub>OH is present in the medium [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 74" title="Aragao GMF, Lindley ND, Uribelarrea JL, Pareilleux A (1996) Maintaining a controlled residual growth capacity increases the production of polyhydroxyalkanoate copolymers by Alcaligenes eutrophus. Biotechnol Lett 18:8: 937–942. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00154625&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR74" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e723">74</a>]. Commercial copolymers include P(3HB-<i>co</i>−4HB) produced using <i>Cupriavidus necator</i> by Metabolix, USA, P(3HB-<i>co</i>−3HHx) produced also using <i>C. necator</i> by Kaneka, Japan and using <i>Aeromonas hydrophila</i> by P&amp;G, China and by Shandong, Lukang [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 55" title="Chen G-Q (2009) A microbial polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA) based bio- and materials industry, Chemical Society Reviews, 10.1039/B812677C 38, 8: 2434–2446, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1039/B812677C&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR55" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e742">55</a>].</p><p>In addition to the above classifications, PHAs can be classified as block copolymers. Block copolymers are macromolecules composed of sequences, or blocks, of chemically distinct repeat units. Hence, PHAs block copolymers are composed of different sets of PHA homopolymers linked together. Possible block copolymer structures include A-B diblock, A-B-A, A-B-C triblock, or (AB)n repeating multiblock [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 75" title="Bates FS, Fredrickson GH (1990) Block Copolymer Thermodynamics: Theory and Experiment. Annu Rev Phys Chem 41:1: 525–557. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.pc.41.100190.002521&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR75" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e748">75</a>]. It has been demonstrated that alternating between different carbon feedstocks leads to the formation of block copolymers; For instance <i>Cupriavidus necator</i> can synthetize poly(3-hydroxybutyrate)-block-poly(3-hydroxybutyrate-<i>co</i>−3-hydroxyvalerate) P(P3HB-b-P3HBV) when valeric acid containing substrate is alternated with non-valeric acid substrate [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 76" title="Mantzaris NV, Kelley AS, Daoutidis P, Srienc F (2002) A population balance model describing the dynamics of molecular weight distributions and the structure of PHA copolymer chains. Chem Eng Sci 57:21: 4643–4663. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/S0009-2509(02)00370-6&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR76" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e757">76</a>].</p><p>This changes on monomer composition affect the overall characteristics of the polymer. Comparison of the thermophysical characteristics between short-chain-length and medium-chain-length polyhydroxyalkanoates are illustrated in Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab1">1</a>.</p><div class="c-article-table" data-test="inline-table" data-container-section="table" id="table-1"><figure><figcaption class="c-article-table__figcaption"><b id="Tab1" data-test="table-caption">Table 1 Comparison of the thermophysical characteristics between short-chain-length and medium-chain-length PHAs</b></figcaption><div class="u-text-right u-hide-print"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="table-link" data-track="click" data-track-action="view table" data-track-label="button" rel="nofollow" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/tables/1" aria-label="Full size table 1"><span>Full size table</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></figure></div><p>PHAs, in general, can be used in medical devices such as suture threads, patches, meshes, implants, tissue engineering scaffolds and controlled drug delivery systems. In particular, due to their elasticity, medium-chain- length PHAs have been reported to be used for soft tissue implants [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 80" title="Ansari S, Sami N, Yasin D, Ahmad N, Fatma T (2021) Biomedical applications of environmental friendly poly-hydroxyalkanoates. Int J Biol Macromol 183:549–563. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijbiomac.2021.04.171&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR80" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e960">80</a>]. Moreover, there is evidence showing that the cytotoxicity of polyhydroxyalkanoates is inversely proportional with the length of the side chain of the monomer (longer side chains are less cytotoxic) making medium-chain- length PHAs better candidates for medical applications [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 81" title="Sun J, Dai Z, Zhao Y, Chen G-Q (2007) In vitro effect of oligo-hydroxyalkanoates on the growth of mouse fibroblast cell line L929. Biomaterials 28(27):3896–3903. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biomaterials.2007.05.011&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR81" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e963">81</a>].</p><p>Mcl-PHAs are specially utilized for cardiac tissue engineering applications and heart valves because elastic materials are more suitable to be used as leaflets inside the tri-leaflet valve [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 81" title="Sun J, Dai Z, Zhao Y, Chen G-Q (2007) In vitro effect of oligo-hydroxyalkanoates on the growth of mouse fibroblast cell line L929. Biomaterials 28(27):3896–3903. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biomaterials.2007.05.011&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR81" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e969">81</a>]. They also mimic better the mechanical properties of soft nerve tissue [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 82" title="Rai R, Keshavarz T, Roether JA, Boccaccini AR, Roy I (2011) Medium chain length polyhydroxyalkanoates, promising new biomedical materials for the future. Mater Sci Engineering: R: Rep 72:3: 29–47. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mser.2010.11.002&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR82" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e972">82</a>]. Scaffolds of the co-polymer P(3HB-<i>co-</i>3HHx) compared against scaffolds with scl-PHAs monomers showed stronger potential to promote differentiation of neural stem cells into neurons and therefore showed biomedical potential for repairing the central nervous system [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 83" title="Xu X-Y et al (2010) The behaviour of neural stem cells on polyhydroxyalkanoate nanofiber scaffolds, Biomaterials, 31, 14: 3967–3975, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biomaterials.2010.01.132&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR83" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e978">83</a>]. However, studies remain limited mainly to P3HO and the co-polymer P(3HB-<i>co</i>−3HHx) which are the only mcl-PHAs in large quantities [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 82" title="Rai R, Keshavarz T, Roether JA, Boccaccini AR, Roy I (2011) Medium chain length polyhydroxyalkanoates, promising new biomedical materials for the future. Mater Sci Engineering: R: Rep 72:3: 29–47. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mser.2010.11.002&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR82" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e985">82</a>].</p><p>One of the main advantages of PHAs is its high biodegradability compared to petroleum-based plastic. For example, a PHA-made water bottle would be expected to completely degrade in 1.5 to 3.5 years in marine environment [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 42" title="Dilkes-Hoffman LS, Lant PA, Laycock B, Pratt S (2019) The rate of biodegradation of PHA bioplastics in the marine environment: A meta-study. Mar Pollut Bull 142:15–24. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.marpolbul.2019.03.020&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR42" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e991">42</a>] while a high-density polyethylene bottle has a marine half-life of 58 years [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 84" title="Chamas A et al (2020) Degradation Rates of Plastics in the Environment. ACS Sustain Chem Eng 8:9: 3494–3511. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/acssuschemeng.9b06635&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR84" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e994">84</a>]. However they also present some disadvantages such as the high cost of production [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 85" title="Kumar M et al (2020) Bacterial polyhydroxyalkanoates: Opportunities, challenges, and prospects. J Clean Prod 263:121500. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jclepro.2020.121500&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR85" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e997">85</a>]. Other advantages and disadvantages are listed below:</p><p><i>Advantages</i>.</p><p>• Less CO<sub>2</sub> emissions and sustainability [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 18" title="Wang Y, Yin J, Chen G-Q (2014) Polyhydroxyalkanoates, challenges and opportunities. Curr Opin Biotechnol 30:59–65. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.copbio.2014.06.001&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1010">18</a>].</p><p>• The industrial production has a low safety risk compared to petroleum-based plastic production which includes flammable and toxic by-products [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 18" title="Wang Y, Yin J, Chen G-Q (2014) Polyhydroxyalkanoates, challenges and opportunities. Curr Opin Biotechnol 30:59–65. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.copbio.2014.06.001&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1016">18</a>].</p><p>• Waste water is non-toxic [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 18" title="Wang Y, Yin J, Chen G-Q (2014) Polyhydroxyalkanoates, challenges and opportunities. Curr Opin Biotechnol 30:59–65. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.copbio.2014.06.001&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1023">18</a>].</p><p><i>Disadvantages</i></p><p>• Not economically competitive compared to petroleum-based plastic [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 18" title="Wang Y, Yin J, Chen G-Q (2014) Polyhydroxyalkanoates, challenges and opportunities. Curr Opin Biotechnol 30:59–65. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.copbio.2014.06.001&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1033">18</a>].</p><p>• The monomer composition of the polymer is difficult to control [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 18" title="Wang Y, Yin J, Chen G-Q (2014) Polyhydroxyalkanoates, challenges and opportunities. Curr Opin Biotechnol 30:59–65. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.copbio.2014.06.001&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1039">18</a>].</p><p>• Difficulties in processing due to their low crystallization process [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 18" title="Wang Y, Yin J, Chen G-Q (2014) Polyhydroxyalkanoates, challenges and opportunities. Curr Opin Biotechnol 30:59–65. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.copbio.2014.06.001&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1045">18</a>].</p><p>• Heavy water consumption [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 18" title="Wang Y, Yin J, Chen G-Q (2014) Polyhydroxyalkanoates, challenges and opportunities. Curr Opin Biotechnol 30:59–65. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.copbio.2014.06.001&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1051">18</a>].</p><p>• Substrate to product conversion is low [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 18" title="Wang Y, Yin J, Chen G-Q (2014) Polyhydroxyalkanoates, challenges and opportunities. Curr Opin Biotechnol 30:59–65. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.copbio.2014.06.001&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1058">18</a>].</p></div></div></section><section data-title="Metabolic pathways and metabolic engineering to optimize medium-chain-length (mcl) PHAs cell production"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec4-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec4">Metabolic pathways and metabolic engineering to optimize medium-chain-length (mcl) PHAs cell production</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec4-content"><p>The monomer carbon chain length composition of mcl-PHAs is directly influenced by the carbon length of the fatty acid provided [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 86" title="Chen J-Y, Song G, Chen G-Q (2006) A lower specificity PhaC2 synthase from Pseudomonas stutzeri catalyses the production of copolyesters consisting of short-chain-length and medium-chain-length 3-hydroxyalkanoates. Antonie Van Leeuwenhoek 89:1: 157–167. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s10482-005-9019-9&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR86" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1069">86</a>]. In each complete β-oxidation cycle, 2 carbons are removed from the fatty acid chain and cycles will continue to take place in the cell until the fatty acid is completely metabolized, as a result, monomers of different sizes will integrate into the PHA polymer to create a heteropolymer. The difficulty of controlling the polymer’s monomer composition is one of the main challenges of mcl-PHAs scale up production. This difficulty is even greater in <i>Pseudomonas</i> because it has several gene copies for each of these β-oxidation pathway enzymes.</p><p>Nevertheless, weakening the β-oxidation pathway, by knocking out one or more copies of one gene, has proven to increase the carbon flux towards enoyl CoA (mcl-PHAs precursor) and avoid the degradation of the fatty acid substrate, which will enhance PHAs accumulation inside the cell [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 87" title="Lu X, Zhang J, Wu Q, Chen G-Q (2003) Enhanced production of poly(3-hydroxybutyrate-co-3-hydroxyhexanoate) via manipulating the fatty acid β-oxidation pathway in E. coli. FEMS Microbiol Lett 221(1):97–101. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/s0378-1097(03)00173-3&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR87" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1078">87</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 88" title="Liu Q, Luo G, Zhou XR, Chen G-Q (2011) Biosynthesis of poly(3-hydroxydecanoate) and 3-hydroxydodecanoate dominating polyhydroxyalkanoates by β-oxidation pathway inhibited Pseudomonas putida. Metab Eng 13:1: 11–17. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ymben.2010.10.004&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR88" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1081">88</a>] and result in more homogenous PHAs in terms of monomer composition [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 88" title="Liu Q, Luo G, Zhou XR, Chen G-Q (2011) Biosynthesis of poly(3-hydroxydecanoate) and 3-hydroxydodecanoate dominating polyhydroxyalkanoates by β-oxidation pathway inhibited Pseudomonas putida. Metab Eng 13:1: 11–17. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ymben.2010.10.004&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR88" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1084">88</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 89" title="Meng D-C, Chen G-Q (2018) Synthetic Biology of Polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA), in Synthetic Biology– Metabolic Engineering, H. Zhao and A.-P. Zeng Eds. Cham: Springer International Publishing, pp. 147–174" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR89" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1087">89</a>].</p><p>The most common method to weaken the β-oxidation pathway is through gene knockout of <i>fadB</i> and <i>fadA</i> genes (See Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig3">3</a>; Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab2">2</a>). However, similar results have been achieved when using the β-oxidation pathway inhibitor acrylic acid (See Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig3">3</a>; Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab2">2</a>) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 90" title="Jiang XJ, Sun Z, Ramsay JA, Ramsay BA (2013) Fed-batch production of MCL-PHA with elevated 3-hydroxynonanoate content. AMB Express 3(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/2191-0855-3-50&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR90" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1112">90</a>]. On the other hand, mcl-PHAs accumulation can also be enhanced by overexpressing genes that are related to the β-oxidation pathway such as <i>phaJ</i>, <i>phaG</i>, <i>fabG</i> and <i>alKk</i> (See Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig3">3</a>; Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab2">2</a>) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 91" title="Vo MT, Lee K-W, Jung Y-M, Lee Y-H (2008) Comparative effect of overexpressed phaJ and fabG genes supplementing (R)-3-hydroxyalkanoate monomer units on biosynthesis of mcl-polyhydroxyalkanoate in Pseudomonas putida KCTC1639. J Biosci Bioeng 106:1: 95–98. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1263/jbb.106.95&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR91" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1134">91</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 92" title="Flores-Sánchez A, Rathinasabapathy A, López-Cuellar Md, Vergara-Porras R B, and, Pérez-Guevara F (2020) Biosynthesis of polyhydroxyalkanoates from vegetable oil under the co-expression of fadE and phaJ genes in Cupriavidus necator. Int J Biol Macromol 164:1600–1607. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijbiomac.2020.07.275&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR92" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1137">92</a>]. No change in monomer composition is expected to take place nor has been observed when overexpressing these enzymes [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 91" title="Vo MT, Lee K-W, Jung Y-M, Lee Y-H (2008) Comparative effect of overexpressed phaJ and fabG genes supplementing (R)-3-hydroxyalkanoate monomer units on biosynthesis of mcl-polyhydroxyalkanoate in Pseudomonas putida KCTC1639. J Biosci Bioeng 106:1: 95–98. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1263/jbb.106.95&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR91" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e1140">91</a>]. However, more research is needed to observe if substrate specificity of these enzymes can also play a role in monomer composition.</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-3" data-title="Fig. 3"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig3" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 3</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig3_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-3-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig3_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 3" loading="lazy" width="685" height="1015"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/figures/3" data-track-dest="link:Figure3 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 3" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-3-desc"><p>Representation of the β-oxidation pathway and β-oxidation pathway-related genes. Genes circled in red have promoted PHAs accumulation when inactivated in several studies (Refer to Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab2">2</a>). Genes circled in green have promoted PHAs accumulation when overexpressed (Refer to Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab2">2</a>)</p></div></div></figure></div><div class="c-article-table" data-test="inline-table" data-container-section="table" id="table-2"><figure><figcaption class="c-article-table__figcaption"><b id="Tab2" data-test="table-caption">Table 2 mcl-PHAs accumulation enhanced by metabolic engineering of the β-oxidation pathway and the β-oxidation pathway-related enzymes</b></figcaption><div class="u-text-right u-hide-print"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="table-link" data-track="click" data-track-action="view table" data-track-label="button" rel="nofollow" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/tables/2" aria-label="Full size table 2"><span>Full size table</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></figure></div><p>More than 5000 species of bacteria distributed in more than 14 bacterial groups including <i>Firmicutes</i>, <i>Bacilli</i>, <i>Clostridia</i>, <i>Actinobacteria</i>, <i>Micrococcal</i>, <i>Streptomycetales</i>, <i>Corynebacterial</i>, <i>Cyanobacteria</i>/<i>Melainabacteria</i> group, <i>Proteobacteria</i>, <i>Alpha proteobacteria</i>, <i>Gamma proteobacteria</i>, <i>Betaproteobacteria</i>, <i>Delta</i>/<i>Epsilon</i> subdivisions and other groups have the capacity of synthetizing PHA [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 66" title="Lu J, Tappel R, Nomura C (2009) Mini-Review: Biosynthesis of Poly(hydroxyalkanoates). J Macromolecular Science® Part C: Polym Reviews 226–248. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1080/15583720903048243&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR66" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2454">66</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 70" title="Liu Z et al (2019) Domain-centric dissection and classification of prokaryotic poly(3-hydroxyalkanoate) synthases. bioRxiv 693432. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1101/693432&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR70" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2457">70</a>]. Even some and halophilic archaea can accumulate it [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 106" title="Wang L et al (2019) Bioinformatics Analysis of Metabolism Pathways of Archaeal Energy Reserves. Sci Rep 9(1034). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-37768-0&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR106" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2461">106</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 107" title="Lane CE, Benton MG (2015) Detection of the enzymatically-active polyhydroxyalkanoate synthase subunit gene, phaC, in cyanobacteria via colony PCR, Molecular and Cellular Probes, 29, 6: 454–460, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mcp.2015.07.001&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR107" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2464">107</a>].</p><p>However, for use in pilot or industrial scale production, reasonably high productivities of PHAs using fed-batch fermentation should exist in a range of (1 g L<sup>−1</sup> h<sup>−1</sup> to 2 g L<sup>−1</sup> h<sup>−1</sup>) for the process to be economically feasible according to Blunt, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 108" title="Blunt W, Levin DB, Cicek N Bioreactor Operating Strategies for Improved Polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) Productivity, Polymers, vol. 10, no. 11, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3390/polym10111197&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR108" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2478">108</a>]. The study of Jiang, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 90" title="Jiang XJ, Sun Z, Ramsay JA, Ramsay BA (2013) Fed-batch production of MCL-PHA with elevated 3-hydroxynonanoate content. AMB Express 3(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/2191-0855-3-50&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR90" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2482">90</a>] using <i>P. putida</i> does fit this criteria and achieves a production of 1.8 g L<sup>−1</sup> h<sup>−1</sup> but most of the studies presented on the table above ( Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab2">2</a>) have a much lower productivity. For example, PHAs production in the study in which <i>Ralstonia eutropha</i> (Now <i>Cupriavidus necator</i>) accumulated 71% of its dry weight of the copolymer P(HB-<i>co</i>−17%-HHx) equivalates to a final ≈ 2 g/l after 72 h of cultivation [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 94" title="Wong Y-M, Brigham CJ, Rha C, Sinskey AJ, Sudesh K (2012) Biosynthesis and characterization of polyhydroxyalkanoate containing high 3-hydroxyhexanoate monomer fraction from crude palm kernel oil by recombinant Cupriavidus necator. Bioresour Technol 121:320–327. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biortech.2012.07.015&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR94" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2505">94</a>] while in the study of Bhatia, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 96" title="Bhatia SK et al (2018) Production of (3-hydroxybutyrate-co-3-hydroxyhexanoate) copolymer from coffee waste oil using engineered Ralstonia eutropha. Bioprocess Biosyst Eng 41:2: 229–235. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00449-017-1861-4&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR96" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2508">96</a>] using <i>Cupriavidus necator</i> as well fed with coffee waste achieved a productivity of approximately only 0.7 g/l in 72 h. This information leads us to believe that some of the studies that already exist are still not good candidates for scale up and industrial production and more optimization work is needed.</p><p>Additionally, there are other strains that have potential as industrial mcl-PHA producers and other genetical modifications, different than β-oxidation modifications, are efficient [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 109" title="Oliveira-Filho ER, Gomez JGC, Taciro MK, Silva LF (2021) Burkholderia sacchari (synonym Paraburkholderia sacchari): An industrial and versatile bacterial chassis for sustainable biosynthesis of polyhydroxyalkanoates and other bioproducts. Bioresour Technol 337:125472. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biortech.2021.125472&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR109" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2517">109</a>]. For example, genes for the consumption of xylose (<i>xylA</i>, <i>xylB</i> and <i>tktA</i>) were overexpressed along with the PhaC from <i>Aeromonas</i> inside the strain <i>Burkholderia sacchari</i> to produce 55.5% CDW of P(HB-<i>co-</i>HHx) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 110" title="Oliveira-Filho ER et al (2022) Engineering Burkholderia sacchari to enhance poly(3-hydroxybutyrate-co-3-hydroxyhexanoate) [P(3HB-co-3HHx)] production from xylose and hexanoate. Int J Biol Macromol 213:902–914. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijbiomac.2022.06.024&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR110" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2539">110</a>].</p></div></div></section><section data-title="PHA synthase (PhaC)"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec5-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec5">PHA synthase (PhaC)</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec5-content"><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec6">Classification</h3><p>PHA synthases are divided into four classes based on their substrate specificity and on their subunit composition. Classes I, III and IV prefer to synthesize scl-PHAs while class II PhaC synthesize mcl-PHAs [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 14" title="Rehm BHA (2003) Polyester synthases: natural catalysts for plastics. Biochem J 376:1: 15–33. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1042/bj20031254&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR14" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2554">14</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 111" title="Mezzolla V, Urso D, O F, Poltronieri P (2018) Role of PhaC Type I and Type II Enzymes during PHA Biosynthesis. Polymers 10(8):10–3390. polym10080910" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR111" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2557">111</a>]. (see Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab3">3</a>) Yet, new studies show a volume of exceptions to this classification. For example, PhaC2 from <i>Pseudomonas stutzeri</i> 1317, (type II PHA synthase) can incorporate several short-chain-length and medium-chain-length monomers into PHAs [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 112" title="Chen J-Y, Liu T, Zheng Z, Chen J-C, Chen G-Q (2006) Polyhydroxyalkanoate synthases PhaC1 and PhaC2 from Pseudomonas stutzeri 1317 had different substrate specificities. FEMS Microbiol Lett 234:2: 231–237. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1574-6968.2004.tb09538.x&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR112" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2566">112</a>]. <i>Chromobacterium</i> sp. USM2, can also utilize 3-hydroxybutyrate (3HB), 3-hydroxyvalerate (3HV), and 3-hydroxyhexanoate (3HHx) monomers [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 113" title="Bhubalan Ket al. (2011) Characterization of the Highly Active Polyhydroxyalkanoate Synthase of Chromobacteriumsp. Strain USM2, Applied and Environmental Microbiology, 77, 9: 2926, 10.1128/AEM.01997-10" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR113" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2573">113</a>] while <i>Aeromonas caviae</i> can synthesize the random co-polymer P(3HB-co-3HHx) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 1" title="Hazenberg W, Witholt B (1997) Efficient production of medium-chain-length poly(3-hydroxyalkanoates) from octane by Pseudomonas oleovorans: economic considerations. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 48:5: 588–596. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s002530051100&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR1" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2579">1</a>].</p><p>Interestingly, the PhaC from <i>Rhodococcus pyridinivorans</i> BSRT1-1 can synthesize the scl-mcl co-polymer P(3HB-co-3HHx) when expressed recombinantly in a <i>C. necator</i> PHA negative strain [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 114" title="Trakunjae C et al Biosynthesis of P(3HB-co-3HHx) Copolymers by a Newly Engineered Strain of Cupriavidus necator PHB– 4/pBBR_CnPro-phaCRp for Skin Tissue Engineering Application, Polymers, vol. 14, no. 19, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3390/polym14194074&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR114" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2591">114</a>]. The PhaC from the scl-PHA industrial strain <i>C. necator</i> can also broaden the range of monomers that is can take and incorporate mcl monomers into the polymer when expressed recombinantly in <i>E. coli.</i></p><p>These important findings suggest that the PhaC specificity is not as strict as previously thought and that the monomer composition is not only dependent on the PhaC’s substrate specificity but also on the specificity of the other enzymes from the pathway.</p><div class="c-article-table" data-test="inline-table" data-container-section="table" id="table-3"><figure><figcaption class="c-article-table__figcaption"><b id="Tab3" data-test="table-caption">Table 3 Polyester synthases can be divided into four classes</b></figcaption><div class="u-text-right u-hide-print"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="table-link" data-track="click" data-track-action="view table" data-track-label="button" rel="nofollow" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/tables/3" aria-label="Full size table 3"><span>Full size table</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></figure></div></div></div></section><section data-title="Structure and sequence"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec7-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec7">Structure and sequence</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec7-content"><p>PHA synthases belong to the <i>α</i>/<i>β</i>-hydrolase superfamily and have only 8 strictly conserved amino acid residues. This so-called <i>α</i>/<i>β</i>-hydrolase fold consists of a characteristic succession of alpha helices and beta strands, usually found in lipases, where the catalytic residues aspartate, histidine and cysteine are. Out of the catalytic triad, the conserved cysteine residue is used as catalytic nucleophile [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 14" title="Rehm BHA (2003) Polyester synthases: natural catalysts for plastics. Biochem J 376:1: 15–33. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1042/bj20031254&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR14" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2642">14</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 115" title="Wittenborn EC, Jost M, Wei Y, Stubbe J, Drennan CL (2016) Structure of the Catalytic Domain of the Class I Polyhydroxybutyrate Synthase from Cupriavidus necator, (in eng). J Biol Chem 291:48: 25264–25277. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M116.756833&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR115" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2646">115</a>].</p><p>This cysteine is embedded in the PhaC box sequence (GS)-X-C-X-(GA)-G (X is an arbitrary amino acid), which is similar to the lipase box G-X-S-X-G proper of lipases, in the active site of the catalytic domain of the protein [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 115" title="Wittenborn EC, Jost M, Wei Y, Stubbe J, Drennan CL (2016) Structure of the Catalytic Domain of the Class I Polyhydroxybutyrate Synthase from Cupriavidus necator, (in eng). J Biol Chem 291:48: 25264–25277. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M116.756833&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR115" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2652">115</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 116" title="Nambu Y, Ishii-Hyakutake M, Harada K, Mizuno S, Tsuge T (2019) Expanded Amino Acid Sequence of the PhaC Box in the Active Center of Polyhydroxyalkanoate Synthases, FEBS Letters, n/a, n/a, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/1873-3468.13651&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR116" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2655">116</a>]. In a posterior amino acid replacement study using PhaC from <i>Cupriavidus necator</i> the PhaC box sequence was proven to be still active when expanded to ([GAST]-X-C-X-[GASV]-[GA]). This study also shows the low mutational robustness of the last glycine residue as well as that of the central cysteine [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 111" title="Mezzolla V, Urso D, O F, Poltronieri P (2018) Role of PhaC Type I and Type II Enzymes during PHA Biosynthesis. Polymers 10(8):10–3390. polym10080910" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR111" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2661">111</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 116" title="Nambu Y, Ishii-Hyakutake M, Harada K, Mizuno S, Tsuge T (2019) Expanded Amino Acid Sequence of the PhaC Box in the Active Center of Polyhydroxyalkanoate Synthases, FEBS Letters, n/a, n/a, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/1873-3468.13651&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR116" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2664">116</a>].</p><p>In <i>C. necator</i>, the PhaC class I active enzyme is a homodimer with only one active catalytic site [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 115" title="Wittenborn EC, Jost M, Wei Y, Stubbe J, Drennan CL (2016) Structure of the Catalytic Domain of the Class I Polyhydroxybutyrate Synthase from Cupriavidus necator, (in eng). J Biol Chem 291:48: 25264–25277. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M116.756833&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR115" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2673">115</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 117" title="Zhang S, Yasuo T, Lenz RW, Goodwin S (2000) Kinetic and Mechanistic Characterization of the Polyhydroxybutyrate Synthase from Ralstonia eutropha, Biomacromolecules, 1, 2: 244–251, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/bm005513c&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR117" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2676">117</a>]. Each monomer is composed of a single polypeptide chain of 65 kDa which contains an N-terminal domain of unknown function (residues 1–200) and a C-terminal catalytic domain where the polymerization takes place (residues 201–589) (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig4">4</a>, A<b>) (5T6O</b> Protein Data Bank elucidated using x-ray diffraction). The active site of each monomer is separated from the other by 33 Å across an extensive dimer interface. The opening in the substrate access channel of the enzyme is near two arginine residues (one from each chain of the dimer), from which Arg<sub>398</sub> is strictly conserved in class I PhaCs. This arginine along with the His<sub>481</sub> are believed to be important for substrate HB-CoA binding and stabilizing while Arg<sub>421</sub> may be involved in chain termination [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 115" title="Wittenborn EC, Jost M, Wei Y, Stubbe J, Drennan CL (2016) Structure of the Catalytic Domain of the Class I Polyhydroxybutyrate Synthase from Cupriavidus necator, (in eng). J Biol Chem 291:48: 25264–25277. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M116.756833&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR115" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2692">115</a>].</p><p>Figure 4B shows the structure of 3, class II, aligned PhaCs (PmePhaC, PpuPhaC and PstzPhaC) from <i>P. mendocina</i> NK-01, <i>P. putida</i> KT2440 and <i>P. stutzeri</i> 1317 respectively. The catalytic triad Cys, Asp and His are coloured</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-4" data-title="Fig. 4"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig4" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 4</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig4_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-4-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig4_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 4" loading="lazy" width="685" height="788"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/figures/4" data-track-dest="link:Figure4 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 4" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-4-desc"><p>(A) The catalytic domain of PHA synthase from C. necator. Catalytic triad is presented in green. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 115" title="Wittenborn EC, Jost M, Wei Y, Stubbe J, Drennan CL (2016) Structure of the Catalytic Domain of the Class I Polyhydroxybutyrate Synthase from Cupriavidus necator, (in eng). J Biol Chem 291:48: 25264–25277. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M116.756833&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR115" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2720">115</a>] Molecule modelled in PyMOL for this work [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 118" title="PyMOL (2020) [Online]. Available: &#xA;                  http://www.pymol.org/pymol&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR118" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2723">118</a>]. ( 5T6O Protein Data Bank elucidated using x-ray diffraction) (B) Pymol structure of 3 aligned PhaCs (PmePhaC, PpuPhaC and PstzPhaC) from P. mendocina NK-01, P. putida KT2440 and P. stutzeri 1317 respectively. The catalytic triad Cys, Asp and His are coloured.</p></div></div></figure></div><p>The catalytic domain of PHA synthase from <i>Cupriavidus necator</i> shares 29% sequence identity with the class III PhaC from <i>Allochromatium vinosum</i>, which suggests structural similarity with class III synthases. The catalytic domains of class II (<i>Pseudomonas</i>, Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig4">4</a>B)) and IV PhaCs, despite of have being a lot less characterized, are also likely to be homologous, sharing around 40% and 30% identity with <i>C. necator</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 115" title="Wittenborn EC, Jost M, Wei Y, Stubbe J, Drennan CL (2016) Structure of the Catalytic Domain of the Class I Polyhydroxybutyrate Synthase from Cupriavidus necator, (in eng). J Biol Chem 291:48: 25264–25277. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M116.756833&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR115" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2753">115</a>].</p></div></div></section><section data-title="Substrate specificity and kinetics"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec8-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec8">Substrate specificity and kinetics</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec8-content"><p>Comparing K<sub>m</sub> of different substrates gives some clues to the specificity of an enzyme. In the study of Zhang, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 117" title="Zhang S, Yasuo T, Lenz RW, Goodwin S (2000) Kinetic and Mechanistic Characterization of the Polyhydroxybutyrate Synthase from Ralstonia eutropha, Biomacromolecules, 1, 2: 244–251, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/bm005513c&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR117" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2767">117</a>]. the much larger K<sub>m</sub> value for 4HB-CoA compared with 3HB-CoA and 3HV-CoA indicates the importance of the distance between the hydroxyl group and the CoA moiety for substrate binding in the PhaC of <i>C. necator</i>, however, a longer side chain does not seem to strongly affect the substrate binding to PHA synthase of <i>C. necator</i>. (for comparison of more substrates and its K<sub>m</sub> read Table II from Yuan, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 119" title="Yuan W et al (2001) Class I and III Polyhydroxyalkanoate Synthases from Ralstonia eutropha and Allochromatium vinosum: Characterization and Substrate Specificity Studies. Arch Biochem Biophys 394:1: 87–98. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1006/abbi.2001.2522&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR119" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2781">119</a>].) It is also important to mention that, while the natural substrate of PhaC in <i>C. necator</i> is 3HB-CoA, it has been able to accept 3-hydroxyoctanoate-CoA and 3-hydroxydodecanoate-CoA to produce medium-chain-length PHAs when expressed in <i>E. coli</i> suggesting that it might have a broader substrate specificity than previously found [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 72" title="Antonio RV, Steinbüchel A, Rehm BHA (2000) Analysis of in vivo substrate specificity of the PHA synthase from Ralstonia eutropha: formation of novel copolyesters in recombinant Escherichia coli. FEMS Microbiol Lett 182(1):111–117. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1574-6968.2000.tb08883.x&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR72" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2790">72</a>].</p><p><b>Point mutations that might affect substrate specificity</b>.</p><p>Proven PHA producing <i>Pseudomonas</i> strains include: <i>Pseudomonas mosselii</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 120" title="Liu M-H, Chen Y Jr., Lee C-Y (2018) Characterization of medium-chain-length polyhydroxyalkanoate biosynthesis by Pseudomonas mosselii TO7 using crude glycerol, Bioscience, Biotechnology, and Biochemistry, 82, 3: 532–539, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1080/09168451.2017.1422386&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR120" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2807">120</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 121" title="Chen Y Jr., Huang Y-C, Lee C-Y (2014) Production and characterization of medium-chain-length polyhydroxyalkanoates by Pseudomonas mosselii TO7. J Biosci Bioeng 118:2: 145–152. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbiosc.2014.01.012&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR121" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2810">121</a>], <i>Pseudomonas corrugata</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Licciardello G, Catara AF, Catara V Production of Polyhydroxyalkanoates and Extracellular Products Using Pseudomonas Corrugata and P. Mediterranea: A Review, Bioengineering, vol. 6, no. 4, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3390/bioengineering6040105&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR122" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2817">122</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Licciardello G et al (2017) Transcriptome analysis of Pseudomonas mediterranea and P. corrugata plant pathogens during accumulation of medium-chain-length PHAs by glycerol bioconversion. New Biotechnol 37:39–47. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nbt.2016.07.006&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR123" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2817_1">123</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 124" title="Solaiman DKY, Ashby RD, Foglia TA (2002) Physiological Characterization and Genetic Engineering of Pseudomonas corrugata for Medium-Chain-Length Polyhydroxyalkanoates Synthesis from Triacylglycerols. Curr Microbiol 44:3: 189–195. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00284-001-0086-5&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR124" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2820">124</a>], <i>Pseudomonas mediterranea</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 122" title="Licciardello G, Catara AF, Catara V Production of Polyhydroxyalkanoates and Extracellular Products Using Pseudomonas Corrugata and P. Mediterranea: A Review, Bioengineering, vol. 6, no. 4, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3390/bioengineering6040105&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR122" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2826">122</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 123" title="Licciardello G et al (2017) Transcriptome analysis of Pseudomonas mediterranea and P. corrugata plant pathogens during accumulation of medium-chain-length PHAs by glycerol bioconversion. New Biotechnol 37:39–47. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nbt.2016.07.006&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR123" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2829">123</a>], <i>Pseudomonas putida</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Solaiman DKY, Ashby RD, Foglia TA (2002) Physiological Characterization and Genetic Engineering of Pseudomonas corrugata for Medium-Chain-Length Polyhydroxyalkanoates Synthesis from Triacylglycerols. Curr Microbiol 44:3: 189–195. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00284-001-0086-5&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR124" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2836">124</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Sharma PK, Fu J, Zhang X, Fristensky B, Sparling R, Levin DB (2014) Genome features of Pseudomonas putida LS46, a novel polyhydroxyalkanoate producer and its comparison with other P. putida strains. AMB Express 4(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/s13568-014-0037-8&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR125" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2836_1">125</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 126" title="Cerrone F et al (2014) Medium chain length polyhydroxyalkanoate (mcl-PHA) production from volatile fatty acids derived from the anaerobic digestion of grass. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 98:2: 611–620. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-013-5323-x&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR126" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2839">126</a>], <i>Pseudomonas mendocina</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 127" title="Zhao F et al (2019) Screening of endogenous strong promoters for enhanced production of medium-chain-length polyhydroxyalkanoates in Pseudomonas mendocina NK-01. Sci Rep 9(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-39321-z&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR127" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2845">127</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 128" title="Guo W, Duan J, Geng W, Feng J, Wang S, Song C (2013) Comparison of medium-chain-length polyhydroxyalkanoates synthases from Pseudomonas mendocina NK-01 with the same substrate specificity. Microbiol Res 168:4: 231–237. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.micres.2012.11.003&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR128" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2848">128</a>], <i>Pseudomonas chlororaphis</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 129" title="Pereira JR et al (2021) Production of medium-chain-length polyhydroxyalkanoates by Pseudomonas chlororaphis subsp. aurantiaca: Cultivation on fruit pulp waste and polymer characterization. Int J Biol Macromol 167:85–92. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijbiomac.2020.11.162&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR129" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2855">129</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 130" title="Meneses L, Craveiro R, Jesus AR, Reis MAM, Freitas F, Paiva A Supercritical CO2 Assisted Impregnation of Ibuprofen on Medium-Chain-Length Polyhydroxyalkanoates (mcl-PHA). Molecules, 26, 16, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules26164772&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR130" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2858">130</a>], <i>Pseudomonas stutzeri</i> 1317 [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 86" title="Chen J-Y, Song G, Chen G-Q (2006) A lower specificity PhaC2 synthase from Pseudomonas stutzeri catalyses the production of copolyesters consisting of short-chain-length and medium-chain-length 3-hydroxyalkanoates. Antonie Van Leeuwenhoek 89:1: 157–167. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s10482-005-9019-9&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR86" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2864">86</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 131" title="Shen X-W, Shi Z-Y, Song G, Li Z-J, Chen G-Q (2011) Engineering of polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) synthase PhaC2Ps of Pseudomonas stutzeri via site-specific mutation for efficient production of PHA copolymers. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 91:3: 655–665. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-011-3274-7&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR131" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2867">131</a>],<i>Pseudomonas entomophila</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 65" title="Wang Y, Chung A, Chen G-Q (2017) Synthesis of Medium-Chain-Length Polyhydroxyalkanoate Homopolymers, Random Copolymers, and Block Copolymers by an Engineered Strain of Pseudomonas entomophila. Adv Healthc Mater 6:7: 1601017. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/adhm.201601017&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR65" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2874">65</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 102" title="Li M et al (2019) Engineering Pseudomonas entomophila for synthesis of copolymers with defined fractions of 3-hydroxybutyrate and medium-chain-length 3-hydroxyalkanoates. Metab Eng 52:253–262. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ymben.2018.12.007&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR102" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2877">102</a>]., <i>Pseudomonas oleovorans</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 132" title="Kraak MN, Kessler B, Witholt B (1997) In vitro Activities of Granule-Bound Poly[(R)-3-Hydroxyalkanoate] Polymerase C1 of Pseudomonas oleovorans. Eur J Biochem 250:2: 432–439. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1432-1033.1997.0432a.x&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR132" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2883">132</a>] and others.</p><p>For this literature review, an amino acid mutation map for the industrially PHA outstanding strain <i>P. putida</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Weimer A, Kohlstedt M, Volke DC, Nikel PI, Wittmann C (2020) Industrial biotechnology of Pseudomonas putida: advances and prospects. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 104:18: 7745–7766. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-020-10811-9&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR133" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2892">133</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Fontaine P, Mosrati R, Corroler D (2017) Medium chain length polyhydroxyalkanoates biosynthesis in Pseudomonas putida mt-2 is enhanced by co-metabolism of glycerol/octanoate or fatty acids mixtures. Int J Biol Macromol 98:430–435. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijbiomac.2017.01.115&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR134" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2892_1">134</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 135" title="Le Meur S, Zinn M, Egli T, Thöny-Meyer L, Ren Q (2012) Production of medium-chain-length polyhydroxyalkanoates by sequential feeding of xylose and octanoic acid in engineered Pseudomonas putida KT2440. BMC Biotechnol 12(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/1472-6750-12-53&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR135" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2895">135</a>] was built following the following procedure: The amino acid sequence from the strain (NCBI: WP_010955566.1) was aligned against the NCBI database using the online tool BLAST [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 136" title="Camacho C, Coulouris G, Avagyan V, Ma N, Papadopoulos, JB, a K, M TL (2009) BLAST+: architecture and applications. BMC Bioinformatics 10:421" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR136" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e2898">136</a>]. The first 50 strains from the search were selected and using all the mutations observed, a mutation map was built and shown in Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig5">5</a> (Mutations shown in red). The occurrence for each mutation (how many times the amino acid was substituted in our pool of sequences) was also recorded and added on the SUPPLEMENTARY material.</p><p>Additionally, a literature search was conducted to identify point mutations that have already been tested in the laboratory. These mutations are shown in blue in Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig5">5</a> and described in Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab4">4</a>.</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-5" data-title="Fig. 5"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig5" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 5</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig5_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-5-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig5_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 5" loading="lazy" width="498" height="2764"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/figures/5" data-track-dest="link:Figure5 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 5" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-5-desc"><p>Mutation map of Pseudomonas putida. The catalytic triad is squared in blue. <b>Lab tested</b> mutations are represented in <b>blue</b> while <b>blast search</b> mutations are written in <b>red</b>. <i>If more than one amino acid is possible in that position then the position is represented by the </i><b><i>+ +</i></b> <i>symbol</i></p></div></div></figure></div><div class="c-article-table" data-test="inline-table" data-container-section="table" id="table-4"><figure><figcaption class="c-article-table__figcaption"><b id="Tab4" data-test="table-caption">Table 4 Point mutations in Pseudomonas that affect substrate specificity</b></figcaption><div class="u-text-right u-hide-print"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="table-link" data-track="click" data-track-action="view table" data-track-label="button" rel="nofollow" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/tables/4" aria-label="Full size table 4"><span>Full size table</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></figure></div><p>Substrate specificity can rely in as much as one amino acid., the substitution of Leu484 in PhaC from <i>Pseudomonas putida</i> for valine shifts the substrate specificity from 8 C to 4 C. This amino acid (484 amino acid) is adjacent to the catalytic triad and it is conserved as leucine in class II synthases, as valine in class III and as valine or isoleucine in class I (<i>C. necator</i>) and is critical in determining substrate specificity [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 138" title="Chen Y Jr., Tsai P-C, Hsu C-H, Lee C-Y (2014) Critical residues of class II PHA synthase for expanding the substrate specificity and enhancing the biosynthesis of polyhydroxyalkanoate, Enzyme and Microbial Technology, 56: 60–66, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.enzmictec.2014.01.005&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR138" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e3569">138</a>]. On the other hand, amino acids Ser326 and Gln482 (also near the catalytic triad) in <i>Pseudomonas spp.</i> are conserved residues, that when substituted for other residues (thr326, cys326 and lys482, arg482 respectively) have a contrary effect and increase the enzyme preference for producing PHB (C4) over mcl-polymers [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 131" title="Shen X-W, Shi Z-Y, Song G, Li Z-J, Chen G-Q (2011) Engineering of polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) synthase PhaC2Ps of Pseudomonas stutzeri via site-specific mutation for efficient production of PHA copolymers. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 91:3: 655–665. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-011-3274-7&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR131" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e3575">131</a>]. The reason why different amino acids can change the enzyme properties in different ways is probably because every amino acid has different physicochemical properties such as hydrophobicity, charge, isoelectric point etc. In this regard Chuah, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 146" title="Chuah J-A et al (2013) Characterization of Site-Specific Mutations in a Short-Chain-Length/Medium-Chain-Length Polyhydroxyalkanoate Synthase: In Vivo and In Vitro Studies of Enzymatic Activity and Substrate Specificity. Appl Environ Microbiol 79:12: 3813–3821. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1128/AEM.00564&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR146" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e3579">146</a>] noticed that substitutions with uncharged residues resulted in enhanced PHA production.</p><p>Conservative mutations occur when an amino acid is substituted for another with similar physicochemical characteristics while non-conservative mutations occur when the amino acid change for another with completely different characteristics [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 147" title="Seng Wong T, Lan Tee K, Springer (eds) (2020) A Practical Guide to Protein Engineering" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR147" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e3585">147</a>]. Non-conservative mutations are expected to have a bigger impact over the protein structure/function.</p><p>Another important characteristic of amino acids is their size and structure. In a study using the class I PhaC from <i>Chromobacterium</i> (short-chain length preference), the mutation A479S resulted in a 4-fold increase in 3HHx monomer incorporation and a 1.6-fold increase in PHA biosynthesis. Further analysis suggested that a change in size and the geometry of the substrate-binding pocket due to this mutation was determinant for PhaC yield and specificity [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 146" title="Chuah J-A et al (2013) Characterization of Site-Specific Mutations in a Short-Chain-Length/Medium-Chain-Length Polyhydroxyalkanoate Synthase: In Vivo and In Vitro Studies of Enzymatic Activity and Substrate Specificity. Appl Environ Microbiol 79:12: 3813–3821. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1128/AEM.00564&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR146" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e3594">146</a>].</p><p>Interestingly enough, the amino acid A479 is also present in PhaC from <i>Cupriavidus necator</i> H16 which is also a class I PhaC. This suggests that this mutation would most likely change the substrate specificity of this enzyme as well but experimental work is needed to confirm this hypothesis.</p><p>P<b>rotein engineering for catalytic enhancement</b>.</p><p>PHA synthase engineering is being performed for a variety of purposes that include improved PHAs accumulation yield, substrate specificity modification and/or higher molecular weight [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 148" title="Tsuge T (2016) Fundamental factors determining the molecular weight of polyhydroxyalkanoate during biosynthesis. Polym J 48:11: 1051–1057. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1038/pj.2016.78&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR148" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e3613">148</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 149" title="Zou H, Shi M, Zhang T, Li L, Li L, Xian M (2017) Natural and engineered polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) synthase: key enzyme in biopolyester production. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 101:20: 7417–7426. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-017-8485-0&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR149" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e3616">149</a>].</p><p>The engineering strategies for these purposes include random mutagenesis, error-prone PCR mutagenesis, site-specific saturation mutagenesis, localized semi-random mutagenesis, gene shuffling, recombination of beneficial mutations and engineering of chimeric PHA synthases between others [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 149" title="Zou H, Shi M, Zhang T, Li L, Li L, Xian M (2017) Natural and engineered polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) synthase: key enzyme in biopolyester production. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 101:20: 7417–7426. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-017-8485-0&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR149" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e3622">149</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 150" title="Nomura CT, Taguchi S (2007) PHA synthase engineering toward superbiocatalysts for custom-made biopolymers. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 73:5: 969–979. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-006-0566-4&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR150" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e3625">150</a>]. Examples of enhanced PhaCs by enzyme evolution are shown in Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab5">5</a>.</p><div class="c-article-table" data-test="inline-table" data-container-section="table" id="table-5"><figure><figcaption class="c-article-table__figcaption"><b id="Tab5" data-test="table-caption">Table 5 Studies of PhaC enhancement through synthetic PHA synthase evolution</b></figcaption><div class="u-text-right u-hide-print"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="table-link" data-track="click" data-track-action="view table" data-track-label="button" rel="nofollow" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/tables/5" aria-label="Full size table 5"><span>Full size table</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></figure></div><p>In addition to the above, PHA yield can be enhanced indirectly by modifying metabolic routes, for instance, the <i>β</i>-oxidation pathway to produce mcl-PHAs [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 66" title="Lu J, Tappel R, Nomura C (2009) Mini-Review: Biosynthesis of Poly(hydroxyalkanoates). J Macromolecular Science® Part C: Polym Reviews 226–248. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1080/15583720903048243&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR66" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4373">66</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 89" title="Meng D-C, Chen G-Q (2018) Synthetic Biology of Polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA), in Synthetic Biology– Metabolic Engineering, H. Zhao and A.-P. Zeng Eds. Cham: Springer International Publishing, pp. 147–174" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR89" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4376">89</a>], by recycling the CoA released from the transformation of 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA into PHAs (CoA inhibits PhaC) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 160" title="Han X, Satoh Y, Tajima K, Matsushima T, Munekata M (2009) Chemo-enzymatic synthesis of polyhydroxyalkanoate by an improved two-phase reaction system (TPRS). J Biosci Bioeng 108:6: 517–523. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbiosc.2009.06.004&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR160" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4379">160</a>] or by the co-expression of molecular chaperones, which results in the synthesis of larger quantities of enzyme [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 161" title="Thomson N Met al. (2013) Efficient Production of Active Polyhydroxyalkanoate Synthase in Escherichia coliby Coexpression of Molecular Chaperones, Applied and Environmental Microbiology, 79, 6: 1948, 10.1128/AEM.02881-12" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR161" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4382">161</a>]. Another approach would be to use thermo-tolerant PHA synthases in order to enhance the bioconversion process and decrease the energy costs related with managing the exothermic fermentation process [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 149" title="Zou H, Shi M, Zhang T, Li L, Li L, Xian M (2017) Natural and engineered polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) synthase: key enzyme in biopolyester production. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 101:20: 7417–7426. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-017-8485-0&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR149" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4386">149</a>].</p></div></div></section><section data-title="Methods to evaluate the enzymatic activity"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec9-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec9">Methods to evaluate the enzymatic activity</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec9-content"><p>The activity of PHA synthase can be determined by measuring the amount of CoA released from thioester-CoA during polymerization. The CoA in the medium is detected spectroscopically at 412 nm by reduction of 5,5-dithio-bis (2 nitrobenzoic acid) (DTNB), a compound which specifically reacts with thiol groups. One unit of enzyme activity is defined as the amount of enzyme that catalyses the release of 1 mol CoA/min. This technique, originally developed for short-chain-length PHA synthases was then modified for medium-chain-length synthases [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 162" title="Valentin HE, Steinbüchel A (1994) Application of enzymatically synthesized short-chain-length hydroxy fatty acid coenzyme A thioesters for assay of polyhydroxyalkanoic acid synthases. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 40:5: 699–709. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00173332&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR162" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4397">162</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 163" title="de Roo G, Ren Q, Witholt B, Kessler B (2000) Development of an improved in vitro activity assay for medium chain length PHA polymerases based on CoenzymeA release measurements. J Microbiol Methods 41(1):1–8. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/S0167-7012(00)00129-9&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR163" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4400">163</a>]. It is the most common technique and it is quick to perform, unfortunately, hydrolysis reactions can lead to the release of CoA independently of polymerisation which can give false results [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 71" title="Thomson N, Roy I, Summers D, Sivaniah E (2010) In vitro production of polyhydroxyalkanoates: achievements and applications. J Chem Technol Biotechnol 85:6: 760–767. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/jctb.2299&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR71" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4403">71</a>].</p><p>Another way to measure PHA synthase activity is by measuring, spectrophotometrically at 236 nm, the hydrolysis of thioesters in substrates as described by Fukui, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 164" title="Fukui T et al (1976) Enzymatic synthesis of poly-β-hydroxybutyrate inZoogloea ramigera. Arch Microbiol 110:2: 149–156. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00690222&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR164" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4409">164</a>]. In this technique one unit of enzyme activity is defined as the amount of enzyme necessary to convert 1 µmol of substrate to PHAs in one minute. It is a convenient technique but less accurate than measuring CoA release [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 71" title="Thomson N, Roy I, Summers D, Sivaniah E (2010) In vitro production of polyhydroxyalkanoates: achievements and applications. J Chem Technol Biotechnol 85:6: 760–767. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/jctb.2299&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR71" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4412">71</a>]. Later on, Gerngross, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 165" title="Gerngross TU et al (1994) Overexpression and Purification of the Soluble Polyhydroxyalkanoate Synthase from Alcaligenes eutrophus: Evidence for a Required Posttranslational Modification for Catalytic Activity, Biochemistry, 33, 31: 9311–9320, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/bi00197a035&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR165" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4415">165</a>] modified the technique and used a labelled substrate ([3-<sup>3</sup>H]-hydroxyacyl-CoAs) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 71" title="Thomson N, Roy I, Summers D, Sivaniah E (2010) In vitro production of polyhydroxyalkanoates: achievements and applications. J Chem Technol Biotechnol 85:6: 760–767. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/jctb.2299&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR71" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4420">71</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 165" title="Gerngross TU et al (1994) Overexpression and Purification of the Soluble Polyhydroxyalkanoate Synthase from Alcaligenes eutrophus: Evidence for a Required Posttranslational Modification for Catalytic Activity, Biochemistry, 33, 31: 9311–9320, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/bi00197a035&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR165" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4424">165</a>].</p><p>In the other hand, the enzyme activity essay developed by Kraak, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 132" title="Kraak MN, Kessler B, Witholt B (1997) In vitro Activities of Granule-Bound Poly[(R)-3-Hydroxyalkanoate] Polymerase C1 of Pseudomonas oleovorans. Eur J Biochem 250:2: 432–439. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1432-1033.1997.0432a.x&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR132" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4430">132</a>] is based on the analysis of substrate depletion by HPLC and production formation by gas chromatography and has proven to be highly accurate to measure the enzyme activity of medium chain length PHA synthases, but, the method is very time consuming.</p><p>The reliance on spectrophotometric assays has the drawback of hindering the simultaneous measurement of more than one enzyme activity [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 71" title="Thomson N, Roy I, Summers D, Sivaniah E (2010) In vitro production of polyhydroxyalkanoates: achievements and applications. J Chem Technol Biotechnol 85:6: 760–767. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/jctb.2299&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR71" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4436">71</a>]. Burns, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 166" title="Burns KL, Oldham CD, Thompson JR, Lubarsky M, May SW (2007) Analysis of the in vitro biocatalytic production of poly-(β)-hydroxybutyric acid, Enzyme and Microbial Technology, 41, 5: 591–599, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.enzmictec.2007.05.003&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR166" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4439">166</a>] developed a HPLC methodology to measure fluctuations in the concentration of CoA, acetyl-CoA, acetoacetyl-CoA, and β-hydroxybutyryl-CoA metabolites and to associate these fluctuations to the activity of an enzyme on the overall system.</p><p>A summary of the methods discussed in this section is shown in Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab6">6</a>.</p><div class="c-article-table" data-test="inline-table" data-container-section="table" id="table-6"><figure><figcaption class="c-article-table__figcaption"><b id="Tab6" data-test="table-caption">Table 6 Different methods to analyse PhaC activity</b></figcaption><div class="u-text-right u-hide-print"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="table-link" data-track="click" data-track-action="view table" data-track-label="button" rel="nofollow" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/tables/6" aria-label="Full size table 6"><span>Full size table</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></figure></div><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec10">PHA depolymerase (PhaZ)</h3><h4 class="c-article__sub-heading c-article__sub-heading--small" id="Sec11">Classification</h4><p>PHA depolymerases are carboxylesterases classified in 3 superfamilies according to the place where they are found in the cell (intracellular, extracellular, periplasmic) which at the same time are divided according to their substrate specificity (short or medium-chain-length) and according to their sequence similarities (presence of the lipase box and catalytic domain type).</p><p>They can also be classified according to the type of PHAs they degrade which are, either native intracellular granules (amorphous)(nPHAs) or denatured extracellular granules (crystalline)(ds) as shown in Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig6">6</a> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 6" title="Jendrossek D, Handrick R (2002) Microbial Degradation of Polyhydroxyalkanoates. Annu Rev Microbiol 56:1: 403–432. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.micro.56.012302.160838&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4641">6</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 17" title="Knoll M, Hamm TM, Wagner F, Martinez V, Pleiss J (2009) The PHA Depolymerase Engineering Database: A systematic analysis tool for the diverse family of polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) depolymerases. BMC Bioinformatics 10(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2105-10-89&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR17" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4644">17</a>].</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-6" data-title="Fig. 6"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig6" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 6</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><img aria-describedby="figure-6-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig6_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 6" loading="lazy" width="685" height="521"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/figures/6" data-track-dest="link:Figure6 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 6" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-6-desc"><p>Classification of PHA depolymerases</p></div></div></figure></div><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec12">Structure and sequence</h3><p>PHA depolymerases have a catalytic triad (serine – histidine – aspartic acid) as active site. Similarly to PhaC, the catalytic serine is located in a G-X-S-X-G lipase box that is present in other α/β-hydrolases. Apart from this, a conserved non-catalytic histidine near the oxyanion hole, also present in lipases, can be found in PhaZ. It is important to note that there are also non-lipase box depolymerases [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 6" title="Jendrossek D, Handrick R (2002) Microbial Degradation of Polyhydroxyalkanoates. Annu Rev Microbiol 56:1: 403–432. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.micro.56.012302.160838&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4675">6</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 17" title="Knoll M, Hamm TM, Wagner F, Martinez V, Pleiss J (2009) The PHA Depolymerase Engineering Database: A systematic analysis tool for the diverse family of polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) depolymerases. BMC Bioinformatics 10(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2105-10-89&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR17" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4678">17</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 167" title="Jaeger KE, Steinbüchel A, Jendrossek D (1995) Substrate specificities of bacterial polyhydroxyalkanoate depolymerases and lipases: bacterial lipases hydrolyze poly(omega-hydroxyalkanoates), (in eng). Applied and environmental microbiology 61(8):3113–3118" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR167" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4681">167</a>].</p><p>The best studied PHA depolymerases are dPHAscl depolymerases which contain a short signal peptide, a catalytic domain (containing the oxyanion hole and the lipase box), a short linker domain, and a substrate binding domain [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 17" title="Knoll M, Hamm TM, Wagner F, Martinez V, Pleiss J (2009) The PHA Depolymerase Engineering Database: A systematic analysis tool for the diverse family of polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) depolymerases. BMC Bioinformatics 10(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2105-10-89&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR17" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4687">17</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 168" title="Behrends A, Klingbeil B, Jendrossek D (1996) Poly(3-hydroxybutyrate) depolymerases bind to their substrate by a C-terminal located substrate binding site. FEMS Microbiol Lett 143:2–3. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1574-6968.1996.tb08479.x&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR168" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4690">168</a>]. In dPHAscl with catalytic domain 1 the oxyanion hole can be found in the N-terminal to the lipase box, like lipases, whereas in dPHAscl with catalytic domain 2 the oxyanion hole is found C-terminal to the catalytic triad. Example of microorganisms harbouring dPHAscl with catalytic domain 1 are <i>Sorangium cellulosum</i>,<i> Paracoccus denitrificans</i>,<i> Alcaligenes faecalis and Burkholderia mallei;</i> whereas examples <i>o</i>f microorganisms with catalytic domain type 2 are <i>Alteromonas macleodii</i>,<i> Pseudoalteromonas atlantica</i>,<i> Cupriavidus pinatubonensis</i> and others [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 6" title="Jendrossek D, Handrick R (2002) Microbial Degradation of Polyhydroxyalkanoates. Annu Rev Microbiol 56:1: 403–432. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.micro.56.012302.160838&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4715">6</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 17" title="Knoll M, Hamm TM, Wagner F, Martinez V, Pleiss J (2009) The PHA Depolymerase Engineering Database: A systematic analysis tool for the diverse family of polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) depolymerases. BMC Bioinformatics 10(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2105-10-89&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR17" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4718">17</a>].</p><p>Similarly, there are three types of short linker domain: fibronectin type (i.e. <i>Alcaligenes faecalis</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 169" title="Saito T, Iwata A, Watanabe T (1993) Molecular structure of extracellular poly(3-hydroxybutyrate) depolymerase fromAlcaligenes faecalis T1. J Environ Polym Degrad 1:2: 99–105. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01418202&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR169" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4727">169</a>]), cadherin-like domain (i.e. <i>Pseudomonas stutzeri</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 170" title="Ohura T, Kasuya K-I, and Doi Y (1999) Cloning and Characterization of the Polyhydroxybutyrate Depolymerase Gene of Pseudomonas stutzeri and Analysis of the Function of Substrate-Binding Domains, Applied and Environmental Microbiology, 65, 1: 189. [Online]. Available: http://aem.asm.org/content/65/1/189.abstract" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR170" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4733">170</a>]) and 2 types of substrate binding domain (i.e. <i>Pseudomonas stutzeri</i> has both of these domains [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 170" title="Ohura T, Kasuya K-I, and Doi Y (1999) Cloning and Characterization of the Polyhydroxybutyrate Depolymerase Gene of Pseudomonas stutzeri and Analysis of the Function of Substrate-Binding Domains, Applied and Environmental Microbiology, 65, 1: 189. [Online]. Available: http://aem.asm.org/content/65/1/189.abstract" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR170" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4740">170</a>] ) as shown in Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig7">7</a> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 169" title="Saito T, Iwata A, Watanabe T (1993) Molecular structure of extracellular poly(3-hydroxybutyrate) depolymerase fromAlcaligenes faecalis T1. J Environ Polym Degrad 1:2: 99–105. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01418202&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR169" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4746">169</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 171" title="Papaneophytou CP, Pantazaki AA, Kyriakidis DA (2009) An extracellular polyhydroxybutyrate depolymerase in Thermus thermophilus HB8. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 83:4: 659–668. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-008-1842-2&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR171" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4749">171</a>].</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-7" data-title="Fig. 7"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig7" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 7</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><img aria-describedby="figure-7-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig7_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 7" loading="lazy" width="685" height="210"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/figures/7" data-track-dest="link:Figure7 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 7" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-7-desc"><p>Regions of a PHA depolymerase and their classifications</p></div></div></figure></div><p>The dPHAscl catalytic domain Type II from <i>Penicillium funiculosum</i>, shown in Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig8">8</a> (<b>2D80</b> from Protein Data Bank elucidated using x-ray diffraction).is comprised of a single domain. The catalytic triad residues are Ser39, Asp121 and His155 and the amide groups of Ser40 and Cys250 form the oxyanion hole. A mutant revealed that Trp307 plays a role in the recognition of the ester group adjacent to the scissile group [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 172" title="Hisano T et al (2006) The Crystal Structure of Polyhydroxybutyrate Depolymerase from Penicillium funiculosum Provides Insights into the Recognition and Degradation of Biopolyesters. J Mol Biol 356:4: 993–1004. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmb.2005.12.028&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR172" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4784">172</a>].</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-8" data-title="Fig. 8"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig8" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 8</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig8_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-8-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig8_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 8" loading="lazy" width="685" height="631"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/figures/8" data-track-dest="link:Figure8 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 8" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-8-desc"><p>dPHAscl type II from Penicillium funiculosum. The enzyme has 319 amino acid residues with a molecular mass of 33,569 Da. The enzyme has a α/β hydrolase fold where the catalytic residues are (marked in pink) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 172" title="Hisano T et al (2006) The Crystal Structure of Polyhydroxybutyrate Depolymerase from Penicillium funiculosum Provides Insights into the Recognition and Degradation of Biopolyesters. J Mol Biol 356:4: 993–1004. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmb.2005.12.028&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR172" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4799">172</a>]. Molecule modelled in PyMOL for this work [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 118" title="PyMOL (2020) [Online]. Available: &#xA;                  http://www.pymol.org/pymol&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR118" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4802">118</a>]. (<b>2D80</b> from Protein Data Bank elucidated using x-ray diffraction)</p></div></div></figure></div><p>Crystallization of PHA depolymerase from <i>Paucimonas lemoignei</i>, whose catalytic triad special arrangement is similar to that one from <i>P. funiculosum</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 173" title="Wakadkar S, Hermawan S, Jendrossek D, Papageorgiou AC (2010) The structure of PhaZ7 at atomic (1.2 A) resolution reveals details of the active site and suggests a substrate-binding mode, (in eng), Acta Crystallogr Sect F Struct Biol Cryst Commun, 66, Pt 6: 648–654, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1107/S174430911001434X&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR173" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4825">173</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 174" title="Kellici TF, Mavromoustakos T, Jendrossek D, Papageorgiou AC (2017) Crystal structure analysis, covalent docking, and molecular dynamics calculations reveal a conformational switch in PhaZ7 PHB depolymerase, Proteins: Structure, Function, and Bioinformatics, 85, 7: 1351–1361, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/prot.25296&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR174" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4828">174</a>] and from <i>Bacillus thuringiensis</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 175" title="Wang Y-L, Lin Y-T, Chen C-L, Shaw G-C, Liaw S-H (2014) Crystallization and preliminary crystallographic analysis of poly(3-hydroxybutyrate) depolymerase from Bacillus thuringiensis. Struct biology Commun 70:10: 1421–1423" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR175" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4835">175</a>] have also being achieved.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec13">Substrate specificity and kinetics</h3><p>PHA depolymerase catalyzes the reaction in Fig.<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig9"> 9</a>.</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-9" data-title="Fig. 9"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig9" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 9</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><img aria-describedby="figure-9-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig9_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 9" loading="lazy" width="001" height="001"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/figures/9" data-track-dest="link:Figure9 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 9" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-9-desc"><p>Polyhydroxyalkanoate polymers are degraded to hydroxyalkanoic acid monomers. Modelled using ChemDraw [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 62" title="Evans DA (2014) History of the Harvard ChemDraw Project, Angewandte Chemie International Edition, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/anie.201405820&#xA;                  &#xA;                , 42: 11140–11145." href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR62" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4861">62</a>]</p></div></div></figure></div><p>PHA depolymerization occurs in two steps: adsorption by the substrate binding domain and then hydrolysis by the catalytic domain [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 176" title="Mukai K, Yamada K, Doi Y (1993) Kinetics and mechanism of heterogeneous hydrolysis of poly[(R)-3-hydroxybutyrate] film by PHA depolymerases. Int J Biol Macromol 15:6: 361–366. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/0141-8130(93)90054-P&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR176" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4875">176</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 177" title="Kasuya K-i, Inoue Y, Doi Y (1996) Adsorption kinetics of bacterial PHB depolymerase on the surface of polyhydroxyalkanoate films. Int J Biol Macromol 19:1: 35–40. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/0141-8130(96)01097-5&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR177" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4878">177</a>]. The C-terminal amino acids of dPHAscl depolymerases represent the PHA-specific binding domain to the substrate (SBD) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 168" title="Behrends A, Klingbeil B, Jendrossek D (1996) Poly(3-hydroxybutyrate) depolymerases bind to their substrate by a C-terminal located substrate binding site. FEMS Microbiol Lett 143:2–3. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1574-6968.1996.tb08479.x&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR168" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4881">168</a>]. SBD has an interaction of 100 pN with the substrate [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 178" title="Fujita M et al (2005) Interaction between Poly[(R)-3-hydroxybutyrate] Depolymerase and Biodegradable Polyesters Evaluated by Atomic Force Microscopy, Langmuir, 21, 25: 11829–11835, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/la051903e&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR178" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4884">178</a>] and such interaction consists of hydrogen bonds between hydrophilic residues in the enzyme and ester bonds in the polymer and between the hydrophobic residues in the enzyme and the methyl groups in the polymer. Check Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig10">10</a> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 179" title="Hiraishi T, Hirahara Y, Doi Y, Maeda M, and Taguchi S (2006) Effects of Mutations in the Substrate-Binding Domain of Poly[(R)-3-Hydroxybutyrate](PHB) Depolymerase from Ralstonia pickettii T1 on PHB Degradation,Applied and Environmental Microbiology, 72, 11: 7331, 10.1128/AEM.01187-06" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR179" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4891">179</a>].</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-10" data-title="Fig. 10"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig10" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 10</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig10_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-10-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10924-024-03474-4/MediaObjects/10924_2024_3474_Fig10_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 10" loading="lazy" width="685" height="306"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/figures/10" data-track-dest="link:Figure10 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 10" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-10-desc"><p>Schematic model of the interaction of the substrate-binding domain with the PHB (Taken from Hiraishi, 2006 [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 179" title="Hiraishi T, Hirahara Y, Doi Y, Maeda M, and Taguchi S (2006) Effects of Mutations in the Substrate-Binding Domain of Poly[(R)-3-Hydroxybutyrate](PHB) Depolymerase from Ralstonia pickettii T1 on PHB Degradation,Applied and Environmental Microbiology, 72, 11: 7331, 10.1128/AEM.01187-06" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR179" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4906">179</a>]). Dashed bonds are used to represent hydrogen bonds whereas hydrophobic interactions are circled</p></div></div></figure></div><p>Using <i>Alcaligenes faecalis</i>, in a study with 5 substrates the SBD was capable of adsorbing on the surface of all films (PHB, PHP, P4HB, P(2HP) and P(6Hx)); However, the enzyme, only hydrolysed 3 films (PHB, PHP and P(4HB) out of the 5, demonstrating that the binding domain works independently of the catalytic domain and that it is less specific [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 177" title="Kasuya K-i, Inoue Y, Doi Y (1996) Adsorption kinetics of bacterial PHB depolymerase on the surface of polyhydroxyalkanoate films. Int J Biol Macromol 19:1: 35–40. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/0141-8130(96)01097-5&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR177" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4924">177</a>].</p><p>In the other hand, dPHAmcl depolymerases do not contain a substrate binding domain. In these enzymes, the N-terminal region of the catalytic domain is thought to function as substrate binding site [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 6" title="Jendrossek D, Handrick R (2002) Microbial Degradation of Polyhydroxyalkanoates. Annu Rev Microbiol 56:1: 403–432. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.micro.56.012302.160838&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4930">6</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 17" title="Knoll M, Hamm TM, Wagner F, Martinez V, Pleiss J (2009) The PHA Depolymerase Engineering Database: A systematic analysis tool for the diverse family of polyhydroxyalkanoate (PHA) depolymerases. BMC Bioinformatics 10(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2105-10-89&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR17" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4933">17</a>].</p><p>As mentioned before, there are two types of catalytic domains in PHA depolymerase depending on the position of the lipase box and oxyanion hole. Nevertheless, comparisons of substrate specificities between <i>Alcaligenes faecalis</i> (domain type 1), <i>Pseudomonas stutzeri</i> (type 1), and <i>Comamonas acidovorans</i> (type 2) using 12 different aliphatic polyesters showed that active sites of PHA depolymerases have a similar conformational structure, independently of a difference in the sequence structure of the catalytic domains given that they were able to degrade the same 5 substrates (PHB, PHP, P(4HB), poly(ethylene-succinate) and poly(ethylene-adipate)) and unable to degrade the other seven [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 180" title="Kasuya K-i, Ohura T, Masuda K, Doi Y (1999) Substrate and binding specificities of bacterial polyhydroxybutyrate depolymerases. Int J Biol Macromol 24(4):329–336. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/S0141-8130(99)00046-X&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR180" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e4948">180</a>].</p><p>The maximum rate of PHA depolymerase catalysed reaction (Vmax) as well as the Km constant for the degradation of PHB at optimal conditions have been measured for several organisms as shown in Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab7">7</a>. A low Km represents a very big affinity for the substrate.</p><div class="c-article-table" data-test="inline-table" data-container-section="table" id="table-7"><figure><figcaption class="c-article-table__figcaption"><b id="Tab7" data-test="table-caption">Table 7 PHA depolymerases and their kinetic parameters using PHB as a substrate</b></figcaption><div class="u-text-right u-hide-print"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="table-link" data-track="click" data-track-action="view table" data-track-label="button" rel="nofollow" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/tables/7" aria-label="Full size table 7"><span>Full size table</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></figure></div><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec14">Protein engineering for catalytic enhancement</h3><p>Engineering of PHA depolymerases can be achieved using the same techniques described for PHA synthase engineering but a lot less research has been made over PhaZ.</p><p>The degradation rate of a piece of PHB is usually on the order of a few months (in anaerobic sewage) or years (in seawater) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 185" title="Madison LL, Huisman GW (1999) Metabolic Engineering of Poly(3-Hydroxyalkanoates): From DNA to Plastic, Microbiology and Molecular Biology Reviews, 63, 1: 21. [Online]. Available: &#xA;                  http://mmbr.asm.org/content/63/1/21.abstract&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR185" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5195">185</a>] but genetical engineering can be applied to make the process more efficient, for example, Hiraishi, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 186" title="Hiraishi T, Komiya N, Maeda M (2010) Y443F mutation in the substrate-binding domain of extracellular PHB depolymerase enhances its PHB adsorption and disruption abilities. Polym Degrad Stab 95:8: 1370–1374. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.polymdegradstab.2010.01.022&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR186" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5198">186</a>] made an amino acid substitution of the residue Tyr443 for a more highly hydrophobic amino acid (Phe) in PhaZ from <i>Ralstonia pickettii</i> which resulted in a higher PHB degradation activity while Tan, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 187" title="Tan L-T, Hiraishi T, Sudesh K, Maeda M (2014) Effects of mutation at position 285 of Ralstonia pickettii T1 poly[(R)-3-hydroxybutyrate] depolymerase on its activities. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 98:16: 7061–7068. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-014-5660-4&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR187" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5204">187</a>] performed directed evolution over the catalytic domain through error- prone PCR to achieve a tenfold increase of the PHB depolymerase activity.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec15">Methods to evaluate the enzymatic activity</h3><p>As mentioned before, one of the most common and simple techniques to identify PHA-producing colonies is staining them with the hydrophobic dye Sudan Black and observing under the microscope. When PHAs consumption conditions are present, colonies expressing intracellular PHA depolymerase will look less stained than before [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 188" title="Huisman GW, Wonink E, Meima R, Kazemier B, Terpstra P, Witholt B (1991) Metabolism of poly(3-hydroxyalkanoates) (PHAs) by Pseudomonas oleovorans. Identification and sequences of genes and function of the encoded proteins in the synthesis and degradation of PHA, Journal of Biological Chemistry, 266, 4: 2191–2198. [Online]. Available: &#xA;                  http://www.jbc.org/content/266/4/2191.abstract&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR188" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5216">188</a>], whereas, a simple way to observe extracellular depolymerase activity is using an agar plate assay (halo formation) as indicated by Schirmer, et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 189" title="Schirmer A, Jendrossek D, Schlegel HG (1993) Degradation of poly(3-hydroxyoctanoic acid) [P(3HO)] by bacteria: purification and properties of a P(3HO) depolymerase from Pseudomonas fluorescens GK13, Applied and Environmental Microbiology, 59, 4: 1220. [Online]. Available: &#xA;                  http://aem.asm.org/content/59/4/1220.abstract&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR189" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5219">189</a>]. The diameter of the resulting clear zone semi-quantitatively indicates the activity of the enzyme. The technique can also be used to screen microorganisms that utilize extracellular PHAs [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 6" title="Jendrossek D, Handrick R (2002) Microbial Degradation of Polyhydroxyalkanoates. Annu Rev Microbiol 56:1: 403–432. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.micro.56.012302.160838&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5222">6</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 190" title="Ramsay BA, Saracovan I, Ramsay JA, Marchessault RH (1994) A method for the isolation of microorganisms producing extracellular long-side-chain poly (β-hydroxyalkanoate) depolymerase. J Environ Polym Degrad 2(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02073481&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR190" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5225">190</a>].</p><p>In the other hand, quantitative methods include the measurement of the 3HB released by, either using 3HB dehydrogenase and hydrazine and measuring spectrophotometrically [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 191" title="Williamson DH, Mellanby J, Krebs HA (1962) Enzymic determination of d(–)-β-hydroxybutyric acid and acetoacetic acid in blood. Biochem J 82:1: 90–96. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1042/bj0820090&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR191" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5231">191</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 192" title="Kobayashi T, Saito T (2003) Catalytic triad of intracellular poly(3-hydroxybutyrate) depolymerase (PhaZ1) in Ralstonia eutropha H16. J Biosci Bioeng 96:5: 487–492. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/S1389-1723(03)70136-4&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR192" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5234">192</a>] or by directly monitoring absorption at 210 nm of carbonyl groups of 3HB monomer and dimers [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 193" title="Kasuya K-i, Inoue Y, Yamada K, Doi Y (1995) Kinetics of surface hydrolysis of poly[(R)-3-hydroxybutyrate] film by PHB depolymerase from Alcaligenes faecalis T1, Polymer Degradation and Stability, 48, 1: 167–174, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/0141-3910(95)00026-I&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR193" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5237">193</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 194" title="Kasuya K et al (1997) Biochemical and molecular characterization of the polyhydroxybutyrate depolymerase of Comamonas acidovorans YM1609, isolated from freshwater, Applied and Environmental Microbiology, 63, 12: 4844. [Online]. Available: &#xA;                  http://aem.asm.org/content/63/12/4844.abstract&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR194" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5240">194</a>]. Another approach to determine PHA depolymerase activity is to take advantage of the esterase activity of the enzyme and use <i>para</i>-nitrophenylalkanoate (pNPA) as substrate. The concentrated p-nitrophenol released by the hydrolysis of pNPB is measured spectrophotometrically at 410 nm [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 171" title="Papaneophytou CP, Pantazaki AA, Kyriakidis DA (2009) An extracellular polyhydroxybutyrate depolymerase in Thermus thermophilus HB8. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 83:4: 659–668. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-008-1842-2&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR171" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5247">171</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 189" title="Schirmer A, Jendrossek D, Schlegel HG (1993) Degradation of poly(3-hydroxyoctanoic acid) [P(3HO)] by bacteria: purification and properties of a P(3HO) depolymerase from Pseudomonas fluorescens GK13, Applied and Environmental Microbiology, 59, 4: 1220. [Online]. Available: &#xA;                  http://aem.asm.org/content/59/4/1220.abstract&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR189" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5250">189</a>].</p><p>Alternatively, there are weight loss measurements in which PHAs lose weight, over time, as consequence of depolymerization. However, this technique is not practical for rapid routine assays [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 195" title="Handrick R et al (2001) A New Type of Thermoalkalophilic hydrolase of Paucimonas lemoignei with high specificity for amorphous polyesters of short chain-length hydroxyalkanoic acids. J Biol Chem 276 September 28:39: 36215–36224. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M101106200&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR195" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5256">195</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 196" title="Jendrossek D (2007) Peculiarities of PHA granules preparation and PHA depolymerase activity determination. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 74(1186). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-007-0860-9&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR196" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5259">196</a>].</p><p>Finally, non-common methods for recording degradation of polyesters include electron microscopy [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 197" title="Molitoris HP, Moss ST, de Koning GJM, Jendrossek D (1996) Scanning electron microscopy of polyhydroxyalkanoate degradation by bacteria. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 46:5: 570–579. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s002530050863&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR197" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5265">197</a>], 1 H-NMR (hydrogen-nuclear magnetic resonance) imaging [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 198" title="Spyros A, Kimmich R, Briese BH, Jendrossek D (1997) H NMR Imaging Study of Enzymatic Degradation in Poly(3-hydroxybutyrate) and Poly(3-hydroxybutyrate-co-3-hydroxyvalerate). Evidence for Preferential Degradation of the Amorphous Phase by PHB Depolymerase B from Pseudomonas lemoignei, Macromolecules, 30, 26: 8218–8225, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/ma971193m&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR198" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5268">198</a>], <sup>13</sup>C-NMR (carbon nuclear magnetic resonance) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 199" title="Oh JS, Choi MH, Yoon S (2005) In vivo 13 C-NMR spectroscopic study of polyhydroxyalkanoic acid degradation kinetics in bacteria. J Microbiol Biotechnol 15:1330–1336" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR199" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5273">199</a>] and tracking radiolabelled polymers (this technique can be used to measure both polymerization and depolymerization of PHAs) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 200" title="Taidi B, Mansfield DA, Anderson AJ (1995) Turnover of poly(3-hydroxybutyrate) (PHB) and its influence on the molecular mass of the polymer accumulated by Alcaligenes eutrophus during batch culture. FEMS Microbiol Lett 129:2–3. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1574-6968.1995.tb07580.x&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR200" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5276">200</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 201" title="De Eugenio LI et al (2006) Biochemical evidence that phaZ gene encodes a specific intracellular medium chain length polyhydroxyalkanoate depolymerase in Pseudomonas putida KT2442: characterization of a paradigmatic enzyme. J Biol Chem. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M608119200&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR201" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5280">201</a>].</p><p>Summary of the methods discussed in this section are shown in Table <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="table anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Tab8">8</a>.</p><div class="c-article-table" data-test="inline-table" data-container-section="table" id="table-8"><figure><figcaption class="c-article-table__figcaption"><b id="Tab8" data-test="table-caption">Table 8 Comparison of common methods to evaluate PhaZ activity</b></figcaption><div class="u-text-right u-hide-print"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="table-link" data-track="click" data-track-action="view table" data-track-label="button" rel="nofollow" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4/tables/8" aria-label="Full size table 8"><span>Full size table</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></figure></div><p><b>Lipases capable of degrading PHAs.</b></p><p>It has been proven that several lipases can degrade PHAs [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 167" title="Jaeger KE, Steinbüchel A, Jendrossek D (1995) Substrate specificities of bacterial polyhydroxyalkanoate depolymerases and lipases: bacterial lipases hydrolyze poly(omega-hydroxyalkanoates), (in eng). Applied and environmental microbiology 61(8):3113–3118" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR167" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5502">167</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 202" title="Tokiwa Y, Calabia BP (2004) Review Degradation of microbial polyesters. Biotechnol Lett 26:15: 1181–1189. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1023/B:BILE.0000036599.15302.e5&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR202" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5505">202</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 203" title="Sharma PK, Mohanan N, Sidhu R, Levin DB (2019) Colonization and degradation of polyhydroxyalkanoates by lipase-producing bacteria. Can J Microbiol 65:6: 461–475. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1139/cjm-2019-0042&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR203" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5508">203</a>]. A comparison study of PhaZ from <i>P. lemoignei</i> and T1 lipase (isolated from the palm <i>Geobacillus zalihae</i>, capable of degrading amorphous P3HB) showed that their active site residues are very well aligned. The enzymes have 21.4% sequence identity and 53.8% sequence similarity only and, yet, their structures are very similar and they both contain the oxyanion pocket and the lipase box pentapeptide (GXSXG) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 204" title="Mohamed RA, Salleh AB, Leow AT, C, Yahaya NM, Abdul Rahman MB (2017) Ability of T1 Lipase to Degrade Amorphous P(3HB): Structural and Functional Study. Mol Biotechnol 59:7: 284–293. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s12033-017-0012-0&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR204" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5518">204</a>], which can explain this phenomenon.</p><p>Most bacterial lipases are capable of hydrolyzing polyesters consisting of an ω-hydroxyalkanoic acid such as poly(6-hydroxyhexanoate) or poly(4-hydroxybutyrate), whereas polyesters containing side chains in the polymer backbone such as poly(3-hydroxybutyrate) and other poly(3-hydroxyalkanoates) are not or only somewhat hydrolyzed [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 167" title="Jaeger KE, Steinbüchel A, Jendrossek D (1995) Substrate specificities of bacterial polyhydroxyalkanoate depolymerases and lipases: bacterial lipases hydrolyze poly(omega-hydroxyalkanoates), (in eng). Applied and environmental microbiology 61(8):3113–3118" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR167" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5524">167</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 203" title="Sharma PK, Mohanan N, Sidhu R, Levin DB (2019) Colonization and degradation of polyhydroxyalkanoates by lipase-producing bacteria. Can J Microbiol 65:6: 461–475. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1139/cjm-2019-0042&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR203" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5527">203</a>]. Great diversity of commercial lipases going from yeast (<i>Candida antartica</i> and <i>C. rugosa</i>), bacteria (<i>Pseudomonas cepacia</i>, <i>P. fluorescens</i>,<i> Chromobacterium viscosum</i>), fungus (<i>Rhizopus arrhizus</i> and <i>R. oryzae</i>), insect (<i>Myagrus javanicus</i>), shrub (<i>Rubus niveus</i>) and animals (porcine pancreas) are capable of degrading P(3HB-<i>co</i>−4HB) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Mok P-S, Ch’ng D H-E, Ong S-P, Numata K, Sudesh K (2016) Characterization of the depolymerizing activity of commercial lipases and detection of lipase-like activities in animal organ extracts using poly(3-hydroxybutyrate-co-4-hydroxybutyrate) thin film. AMB Express 6(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/s13568-016-0230-z&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR205" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5562">205</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Ch’ng DH-E, Sudesh K (2013) Densitometry based microassay for the determination of lipase depolymerizing activity on polyhydroxyalkanoate. AMB Express 3(1). &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/2191-0855-3-22&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="#ref-CR206" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5562_1">206</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 207" title="Rodríguez-Contreras A, Calafell-Monfort M, Marqués-Calvo MS (2012) Enzymatic degradation of poly(3-hydroxybutyrate-co-4-hydroxybutyrate) by commercial lipases. Polym Degrad Stab 97:4: 597–604. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.polymdegradstab.2012.01.007&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR207" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5565">207</a>].</p><p>No efforts have been made to enhance the catalytic activity of lipases to degrade PHAs but they have been immobilized on the surface of intracellular polyhydroxybutyrate (PHB) granules by fusion of the lipase gene with the <i>pha</i>C operon to produce fatty acid alkyl esters such as biodiesel [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 208" title="Mendes AA, Oliveira PC, Vélez AM, Giordano RC, Giordano R, d L, C, de Castro HF (2012) Evaluation of immobilized lipases on poly-hydroxybutyrate beads to catalyze biodiesel synthesis. Int J Biol Macromol 50:3: 503–511. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijbiomac.2012.01.020&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR208" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5574">208</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 209" title="Yang TH, Kwon M-A, Lee JY, Choi J-E, Oh JY, Song JK (2015) In Situ Immobilized Lipase on the Surface of Intracellular Polyhydroxybutyrate Granules: Preparation, Characterization, and its Promising Use for the Synthesis of Fatty Acid Alkyl Esters. Appl Biochem Biotechnol 177:7: 1553–1564. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s12010-015-1836-3&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR209" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5577">209</a>].</p><p><b>Conclusions and future perspectives.</b></p><p>As indicated before in this review, (Fig.<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig3"> 3</a>) the substrate used by PhaC to produce mcl-PHAs is the intermediate of the fatty acid β-oxidation pathway, 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA. Fatty acids of different carbon lengths when fed to the microbial cell are metabolized by this pathway, and each time the cycle is complete the chain loses 2 carbons [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 210" title="Houten SM, Wanders RJA (2010) A general introduction to the biochemistry of mitochondrial fatty acid β-oxidation. J Inherit Metab Dis 33:5: 469–477. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/s10545-010-9061-2&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR210" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5591">210</a>]. As a result, hydroxyacyl-CoA of different chain lengths in the cell can be incorporated to the PHA polymer making the monomer composition very difficult to control.</p><p>It has been proven that the deletion of all the copies of certain β-oxidation genes (fadA, fadB and PhaG for example, shown in Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#Fig3">3</a><b>)</b> to interrupt the pathway can lead to the accumulation of predicted homopolymers [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 211" title="Chung A-L et al (2011) Biosynthesis and Characterization of Poly(3-hydroxydodecanoate) by β-Oxidation Inhibited Mutant of Pseudomonas entomophila L48, Biomacromolecules, 12, 10: 3559–3566, &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/bm200770m&#xA;                  &#xA;                " href="/article/10.1007/s10924-024-03474-4#ref-CR211" id="ref-link-section-d108074897e5602">211</a>]. In the near future, efforts should be made to synthesize and test homopolymers of different chain lengths and to scale up the process for industrial production. Complementary, the upregulation of the genes fabG, fadE and PhaJ from this same pathway increases the metabolic flux to the production of the PHA substrate 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA thus increasing yield.</p><p>Other methods to increase yield and modify PhaC’s substrate preference is through protein engineering. In this review we show a list of point mutations that have been proven to change substrate preference and suggest some others that have not been proven yet for future research.</p><p>Moreover, in this review we move one step ahead and report the main characteristics, structure, classification and protein engineering methods to modify PhaZ which will be needed in the future to accelerate the degradation of PHA products.</p></div></div></section>
                                </div>
                        
                    

                        

                    
                        
                    

                    

                    <section data-title="Data Availability"><div class="c-article-section" id="data-availability-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="data-availability">Data Availability</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="data-availability-content">
              
              <p>No datasets were generated or analysed during the current study.</p>
            </div></div></section><div id="MagazineFulltextArticleBodySuffix"><section aria-labelledby="Bib1" data-title="References"><div class="c-article-section" id="Bib1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Bib1">References</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Bib1-content"><div data-container-section="references"><ol class="c-article-references" data-track-component="outbound reference" data-track-context="references section"><li class="c-article-references__item js-c-reading-companion-references-item" data-counter="1."><p class="c-article-references__text" id="ref-CR1">Hazenberg W, Witholt B (1997) Efficient production of medium-chain-length poly(3-hydroxyalkanoates) from octane by Pseudomonas oleovorans: economic considerations. Appl Microbiol Biotechnol 48:5: 588–596. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s002530051100" data-track="click_references" data-track-action="external reference" data-track-value="external reference" data-track-label="10.1007/s002530051100">https://doi.org/10.1007/s002530051100</a></p><p class="c-article-references__links u-hide-print"><a data-track="click_references" rel="noopener" data-track-label="10.1007/s002530051100" data-track-item_id="10.1007/s002530051100" data-track-value="article reference" data-track-action="article reference" href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s002530051100" aria-label="Article reference 1" data-doi="10.1007/s002530051100">Article</a>&nbsp;
    <a data-track="click_references" rel="nofollow noopener" data-track-label="link" data-track-item_id="link" data-track-value="cas reference" data-track-action="cas reference" href="/articles/cas-redirect/1:CAS:528:DyaK2sXnsl2ku7s%3D" aria-label="CAS reference 1">CAS</a>&nbsp;
    <a data-track="click_references" data-track-action="google scholar reference" data-track-value="google scholar reference" data-track-label="link" data-track-item_id="link" rel="nofollow noopener" aria-label="Google Scholar reference 1" href="http://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?&amp;title=Efficient%20production%20of%20medium-chain-length%20poly%283-hydroxyalkanoates%29%20from%20octane%20by%20Pseudomonas%20oleovorans%3A%20economic%20considerations&amp;journal=Appl%20Microbiol%20Biotechnol&amp;doi=10.1007%2Fs002530051100&amp;volume=48&amp;pages=588-596&amp;publication_year=1997&amp;author=Hazenberg%2CW&amp;author=Witholt%2CB">
                    Google Scholar</a>&nbsp;
                </p></li><li class="c-article-references__item js-c-reading-companion-references-item" data-counter="2."><p class="c-article-references__text" id="ref-CR2">Lenz RW, Marchessault RH (2005) Bacterial Polyesters: Biosynthesis, Biodegradable Plastics and Biotechnology. Biomacromolecules 6:1: 1–8. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1021/bm049700c" data-track="click_references" data-track-action="external reference" data-track-value="external reference" data-track-label="10.1021/bm049700c">https://doi.org/10.1021/bm049700c</a></p><p class="c-article-references__links u-hide-print"><a data-track="click_references" rel="nofollow noopener" data-track-label="10.1021/bm049700c" data-track-item_id="10.1021/bm049700c" data-track-value="article reference" data-track-action="article reference" href="https://doi.org/10.1021%2Fbm049700c" aria-label="Article reference 2" data-doi="10.1021/bm049700c">Article</a>&nbsp;
    <a data-track="click_references" rel="nofollow noopener" data-track-label="link" data-track-item_id="link" data-track-value="cas reference" data-track-action="cas reference" href="/articles/cas-redirect/1:CAS:528:DC%2BD2cXhtVSmu7rP" aria-label="CAS reference 2">CAS</a>&nbsp;
    <a data-track="click_references" rel="nofollow noopener" data-track-label="link" data-track-item_id="link" data-track-value="pubmed reference" data-track-action="pubmed reference" href="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=Retrieve&amp;db=PubMed&amp;dopt=Abstract&amp;list_uids=15638495" aria-label="PubMed reference 2">PubMed</a>&nbsp;
